a:3:{i:0;a:206:{i:0;a:23:{i:0;i:32769;i:1;i:16395;i:2;s:2:"sr";i:3;s:64:"\x43\x44\x30\x30\x31[\x00-\xff]{1,16384}\xff\x43\x44\x30\x30\x31";i:4;i:0;i:5;i:0;i:6;s:1:"s";i:7;s:0:"";i:8;s:3:"iso";i:9;a:2:{i:0;s:3:"iso";i:1;s:3:"udf";}i:10;s:27:"application/x-iso9660-image";i:11;N;i:12;s:19:"ISO Disk Image File";i:13;N;i:14;N;i:15;s:7:"fmt/468";i:16;N;i:17;N;i:18;N;i:19;s:520:"An ISO image is a disk image of an optical disc. In other words, it is an archive file that contains everything that would be written to an optical disc, sector by sector, including the optical disc file system. ISO image files bear the .iso filename extension. The name ISO is taken from the International Organization for Standardization (ISO) ISO 9660 file system used with CD-ROM media, but what is known as an ISO image might also contain a UDF (ISO/IEC 13346) file system (commonly used by DVDs and Blu-ray Discs).";i:20;N;i:21;N;i:22;a:2:{i:0;s:123:"https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/PRONOM/Format/proFormatSearch.aspx?status=detailReport&id=1255&strPageToDisplay=summary";i:1;s:39:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ISO_image";}}i:1;a:23:{i:0;i:257;i:1;i:8;i:2;s:2:"sr";i:3;s:49:"\x75\x73\x74\x61\x72(?:\x00\x30\x30|\x20\x20\x00)";i:4;i:0;i:5;i:0;i:6;s:1:"s";i:7;s:0:"";i:8;s:3:"tar";i:9;a:1:{i:0;s:3:"tar";}i:10;s:17:"application/x-tar";i:11;s:18:"public.tar-archive";i:12;s:19:"Tape Archive Format";i:13;N;i:14;s:3:"tar";i:15;s:9:"x-fmt/265";i:16;N;i:17;s:9:"Aggregate";i:18;N;i:19;s:479:"In computing, tar is a computer software utility for collecting many files into one archive file, often referred to as a tarball, for distribution or backup purposes. The name is derived from "tape archive", as it was originally developed to write data to sequential I/O devices with no file system of their own. The archive data sets created by tar contain various file system parameters, such as name, timestamps, ownership, file-access permissions, and directory organization.";i:20;s:4:"Text";i:21;N;i:22;a:2:{i:0;s:122:"https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/PRONOM/Format/proFormatSearch.aspx?status=detailReport&id=385&strPageToDisplay=summary";i:1;s:45:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tar_(computing)";}}i:2;a:23:{i:0;i:8;i:1;i:66;i:2;s:2:"sr";i:3;s:62:"\x01\x00\x00\x00[\x00-\xff]{22}\x4c\x50[\x00-\xff]{36}\x00\x00";i:4;i:0;i:5;i:0;i:6;s:1:"s";i:7;s:0:"";i:8;s:3:"eot";i:9;a:1:{i:0;s:3:"eot";}i:10;s:29:"application/vnd.ms-fontobject";i:11;N;i:12;s:35:"Embedded OpenType (EOT) File Format";i:13;s:10:"0x00010000";i:14;N;i:15;s:8:"fmt/1382";i:16;N;i:17;s:4:"Font";i:18;N;i:19;s:255:"The Embedded OpenType File Format (EOT) was developed by Microsoft to enable TrueType and OpenType fonts to be linked to web pages for download to render the web page with the font the author desired - https://www.w3.org/Submission/2008/SUBM-EOT-20080305/";i:20;N;i:21;N;i:22;a:3:{i:0;s:123:"https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/PRONOM/Format/proFormatSearch.aspx?status=detailReport&id=2200&strPageToDisplay=summary";i:1;s:47:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Embedded_OpenType";i:2;s:45:"https://www.w3.org/Submission/EOT/#FileFormat";}}i:3;a:23:{i:0;i:8;i:1;i:66;i:2;s:2:"sr";i:3;s:62:"\x01\x00\x02\x00[\x00-\xff]{22}\x4c\x50[\x00-\xff]{36}\x00\x00";i:4;i:0;i:5;i:0;i:6;s:1:"s";i:7;s:0:"";i:8;s:3:"eot";i:9;a:1:{i:0;s:3:"eot";}i:10;s:29:"application/vnd.ms-fontobject";i:11;N;i:12;s:35:"Embedded OpenType (EOT) File Format";i:13;s:10:"0x00010002";i:14;N;i:15;N;i:16;N;i:17;s:4:"Font";i:18;N;i:19;s:255:"The Embedded OpenType File Format (EOT) was developed by Microsoft to enable TrueType and OpenType fonts to be linked to web pages for download to render the web page with the font the author desired - https://www.w3.org/Submission/2008/SUBM-EOT-20080305/";i:20;N;i:21;N;i:22;a:2:{i:0;s:47:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Embedded_OpenType";i:1;s:45:"https://www.w3.org/Submission/EOT/#FileFormat";}}i:4;a:23:{i:0;i:8;i:1;i:66;i:2;s:2:"sr";i:3;s:62:"\x02\x00\x01\x00[\x00-\xff]{22}\x4c\x50[\x00-\xff]{36}\x00\x00";i:4;i:0;i:5;i:0;i:6;s:1:"s";i:7;s:0:"";i:8;s:3:"eot";i:9;a:1:{i:0;s:3:"eot";}i:10;s:29:"application/vnd.ms-fontobject";i:11;N;i:12;s:35:"Embedded OpenType (EOT) File Format";i:13;s:10:"0x00020001";i:14;N;i:15;s:8:"fmt/1383";i:16;N;i:17;s:4:"Font";i:18;N;i:19;s:255:"The Embedded OpenType File Format (EOT) was developed by Microsoft to enable TrueType and OpenType fonts to be linked to web pages for download to render the web page with the font the author desired - https://www.w3.org/Submission/2008/SUBM-EOT-20080305/";i:20;N;i:21;N;i:22;a:3:{i:0;s:123:"https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/PRONOM/Format/proFormatSearch.aspx?status=detailReport&id=2201&strPageToDisplay=summary";i:1;s:47:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Embedded_OpenType";i:2;s:45:"https://www.w3.org/Submission/EOT/#FileFormat";}}i:5;a:23:{i:0;i:8;i:1;i:66;i:2;s:2:"sr";i:3;s:62:"\x02\x00\x02\x00[\x00-\xff]{22}\x4c\x50[\x00-\xff]{36}\x00\x00";i:4;i:0;i:5;i:0;i:6;s:1:"s";i:7;s:0:"";i:8;s:3:"eot";i:9;a:1:{i:0;s:3:"eot";}i:10;s:29:"application/vnd.ms-fontobject";i:11;N;i:12;s:35:"Embedded OpenType (EOT) File Format";i:13;s:10:"0x00020002";i:14;N;i:15;s:8:"fmt/1384";i:16;N;i:17;s:4:"Font";i:18;N;i:19;s:255:"The Embedded OpenType File Format (EOT) was developed by Microsoft to enable TrueType and OpenType fonts to be linked to web pages for download to render the web page with the font the author desired - https://www.w3.org/Submission/2008/SUBM-EOT-20080305/";i:20;N;i:21;N;i:22;a:3:{i:0;s:123:"https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/PRONOM/Format/proFormatSearch.aspx?status=detailReport&id=2202&strPageToDisplay=summary";i:1;s:47:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Embedded_OpenType";i:2;s:45:"https://www.w3.org/Submission/EOT/#FileFormat";}}i:6;a:23:{i:0;i:4;i:1;i:8;i:2;s:1:"s";i:3;s:8:"ftypheic";i:4;i:0;i:5;i:0;i:6;s:1:"s";i:7;s:0:"";i:8;s:4:"heic";i:9;a:1:{i:0;s:4:"heic";}i:10;s:10:"image/heic";i:11;s:11:"public.heic";i:12;s:77:"High Efficiency Image File Format / HEVC (Main or Main Still Picture profile)";i:13;N;i:14;N;i:15;s:8:"fmt/1101";i:16;N;i:17;s:14:"Image (Raster)";i:18;N;i:19;s:415:"The High Efficiency Image File Format (HEIF) is an image format standard defined in ISO/IEC 23008-12 - MPEG-H Part 12. HEIF can store images, image properties, thumbnails and other derivatives, plus image metadata. It can support individual images and image sequences At this time the PRONOM identification signature does not distinguish between individual images and image sequences although this may be desirable.";i:20;N;i:21;N;i:22;a:3:{i:0;s:123:"https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/PRONOM/Format/proFormatSearch.aspx?status=detailReport&id=1909&strPageToDisplay=summary";i:1;s:47:"https://nokiatech.github.io/heif/technical.html";i:2;s:63:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/High_Efficiency_Image_File_Format";}}i:7;a:23:{i:0;i:4;i:1;i:8;i:2;s:1:"s";i:3;s:8:"ftypheix";i:4;i:0;i:5;i:0;i:6;s:1:"s";i:7;s:0:"";i:8;s:4:"heic";i:9;a:1:{i:0;s:4:"heic";}i:10;s:10:"image/heic";i:11;s:11:"public.heic";i:12;s:85:"High Efficiency Image File Format / HEVC (Main 10 or format range extensions profile)";i:13;N;i:14;N;i:15;s:8:"fmt/1101";i:16;N;i:17;s:14:"Image (Raster)";i:18;N;i:19;s:415:"The High Efficiency Image File Format (HEIF) is an image format standard defined in ISO/IEC 23008-12 - MPEG-H Part 12. HEIF can store images, image properties, thumbnails and other derivatives, plus image metadata. It can support individual images and image sequences At this time the PRONOM identification signature does not distinguish between individual images and image sequences although this may be desirable.";i:20;N;i:21;N;i:22;a:3:{i:0;s:123:"https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/PRONOM/Format/proFormatSearch.aspx?status=detailReport&id=1909&strPageToDisplay=summary";i:1;s:47:"https://nokiatech.github.io/heif/technical.html";i:2;s:63:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/High_Efficiency_Image_File_Format";}}i:8;a:23:{i:0;i:4;i:1;i:8;i:2;s:1:"s";i:3;s:8:"ftyphevc";i:4;i:0;i:5;i:0;i:6;s:1:"s";i:7;s:0:"";i:8;s:4:"heic";i:9;a:2:{i:0;s:4:"heic";i:1;s:5:"heics";}i:10;s:19:"image/heic-sequence";i:11;s:11:"public.heic";i:12;s:77:"High Efficiency Image File Format / HEVC (Main or Main Still Picture profile)";i:13;N;i:14;N;i:15;s:8:"fmt/1101";i:16;N;i:17;s:16:"Image (Sequence)";i:18;N;i:19;s:415:"The High Efficiency Image File Format (HEIF) is an image format standard defined in ISO/IEC 23008-12 - MPEG-H Part 12. HEIF can store images, image properties, thumbnails and other derivatives, plus image metadata. It can support individual images and image sequences At this time the PRONOM identification signature does not distinguish between individual images and image sequences although this may be desirable.";i:20;N;i:21;N;i:22;a:3:{i:0;s:123:"https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/PRONOM/Format/proFormatSearch.aspx?status=detailReport&id=1909&strPageToDisplay=summary";i:1;s:47:"https://nokiatech.github.io/heif/technical.html";i:2;s:63:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/High_Efficiency_Image_File_Format";}}i:9;a:23:{i:0;i:4;i:1;i:8;i:2;s:1:"s";i:3;s:8:"ftyphevx";i:4;i:0;i:5;i:0;i:6;s:1:"s";i:7;s:0:"";i:8;s:4:"heic";i:9;a:2:{i:0;s:4:"heic";i:1;s:5:"heics";}i:10;s:19:"image/heic-sequence";i:11;s:11:"public.heic";i:12;s:85:"High Efficiency Image File Format / HEVC (Main 10 or format range extensions profile)";i:13;N;i:14;N;i:15;s:8:"fmt/1101";i:16;N;i:17;s:16:"Image (Sequence)";i:18;N;i:19;s:415:"The High Efficiency Image File Format (HEIF) is an image format standard defined in ISO/IEC 23008-12 - MPEG-H Part 12. HEIF can store images, image properties, thumbnails and other derivatives, plus image metadata. It can support individual images and image sequences At this time the PRONOM identification signature does not distinguish between individual images and image sequences although this may be desirable.";i:20;N;i:21;N;i:22;a:3:{i:0;s:123:"https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/PRONOM/Format/proFormatSearch.aspx?status=detailReport&id=1909&strPageToDisplay=summary";i:1;s:47:"https://nokiatech.github.io/heif/technical.html";i:2;s:63:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/High_Efficiency_Image_File_Format";}}i:10;a:23:{i:0;i:4;i:1;i:8;i:2;s:1:"s";i:3;s:8:"ftypmif1";i:4;i:0;i:5;i:0;i:6;s:1:"s";i:7;s:0:"";i:8;s:4:"heif";i:9;a:1:{i:0;s:4:"heif";}i:10;s:10:"image/heif";i:11;s:11:"public.heif";i:12;s:33:"High Efficiency Image File Format";i:13;N;i:14;N;i:15;s:8:"fmt/1101";i:16;N;i:17;s:14:"Image (Raster)";i:18;N;i:19;s:415:"The High Efficiency Image File Format (HEIF) is an image format standard defined in ISO/IEC 23008-12 - MPEG-H Part 12. HEIF can store images, image properties, thumbnails and other derivatives, plus image metadata. It can support individual images and image sequences At this time the PRONOM identification signature does not distinguish between individual images and image sequences although this may be desirable.";i:20;N;i:21;N;i:22;a:3:{i:0;s:123:"https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/PRONOM/Format/proFormatSearch.aspx?status=detailReport&id=1909&strPageToDisplay=summary";i:1;s:47:"https://nokiatech.github.io/heif/technical.html";i:2;s:63:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/High_Efficiency_Image_File_Format";}}i:11;a:23:{i:0;i:4;i:1;i:8;i:2;s:1:"s";i:3;s:8:"ftypmsf1";i:4;i:0;i:5;i:0;i:6;s:1:"s";i:7;s:0:"";i:8;s:4:"heif";i:9;a:2:{i:0;s:4:"heif";i:1;s:5:"heifs";}i:10;s:19:"image/heif-sequence";i:11;s:11:"public.heif";i:12;s:33:"High Efficiency Image File Format";i:13;N;i:14;N;i:15;s:8:"fmt/1101";i:16;N;i:17;s:16:"Image (Sequence)";i:18;N;i:19;s:415:"The High Efficiency Image File Format (HEIF) is an image format standard defined in ISO/IEC 23008-12 - MPEG-H Part 12. HEIF can store images, image properties, thumbnails and other derivatives, plus image metadata. It can support individual images and image sequences At this time the PRONOM identification signature does not distinguish between individual images and image sequences although this may be desirable.";i:20;N;i:21;N;i:22;a:3:{i:0;s:123:"https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/PRONOM/Format/proFormatSearch.aspx?status=detailReport&id=1909&strPageToDisplay=summary";i:1;s:47:"https://nokiatech.github.io/heif/technical.html";i:2;s:63:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/High_Efficiency_Image_File_Format";}}i:12;a:23:{i:0;i:1;i:1;i:16;i:2;s:2:"sr";i:3;s:82:"\x01[\x01\x09][\x00-\xff]{4}[\x0f\x10\x18\x20][\x00-\xff]{8}[\x08\x0f\x10\x18\x20]";i:4;i:0;i:5;i:0;i:6;s:1:"s";i:7;s:0:"";i:8;s:3:"tga";i:9;a:6:{i:0;s:3:"afi";i:1;s:3:"bpx";i:2;s:3:"icb";i:3;s:3:"tga";i:4;s:3:"vda";i:5;s:3:"vst";}i:10;s:11:"image/x-tga";i:11;s:24:"com.truevision.tga-image";i:12;s:21:"Truevision TGA Bitmap";i:13;s:11:"1.0 (BOF 1)";i:14;s:17:"TGA, Targa Bitmap";i:15;s:9:"x-fmt/367";i:16;N;i:17;s:14:"Image (Raster)";i:18;N;i:19;s:514:"The Truevision TGA File Format comprises 5 areas, each of which contains one or more fields of fixed or variable length. The 5 file areas are: (1) TGA File Header, (2) Image/Color Map Data, (3) Developer Area, (4) Extension Area and (5) TGA File Footer. The last 3 areas, the Developer Area, the Extension Area and the TGA File Footer are new to the file specification as of September, 1989. For this reason, images created with software written before September, 1989 will probably not contain these three fields.";i:20;s:4:"Text";i:21;N;i:22;a:3:{i:0;s:122:"https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/PRONOM/Format/proFormatSearch.aspx?status=detailReport&id=533&strPageToDisplay=summary";i:1;s:44:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Truevision_TGA";i:2;s:67:"http://www.dca.fee.unicamp.br/~martino/disciplinas/ea978/tgaffs.pdf";}}i:13;a:23:{i:0;i:1;i:1;i:16;i:2;s:2:"sr";i:3;s:78:"\x00[\x02\x03\x0a\x0b]\x00\x00\x00\x00\x00[\x00-\xff]{8}[\x08\x0f\x10\x18\x20]";i:4;i:0;i:5;i:0;i:6;s:1:"s";i:7;s:0:"";i:8;s:3:"tga";i:9;a:6:{i:0;s:3:"afi";i:1;s:3:"bpx";i:2;s:3:"icb";i:3;s:3:"tga";i:4;s:3:"vda";i:5;s:3:"vst";}i:10;s:11:"image/x-tga";i:11;s:24:"com.truevision.tga-image";i:12;s:21:"Truevision TGA Bitmap";i:13;s:11:"1.0 (BOF 2)";i:14;s:17:"TGA, Targa Bitmap";i:15;s:9:"x-fmt/367";i:16;N;i:17;s:14:"Image (Raster)";i:18;N;i:19;s:514:"The Truevision TGA File Format comprises 5 areas, each of which contains one or more fields of fixed or variable length. The 5 file areas are: (1) TGA File Header, (2) Image/Color Map Data, (3) Developer Area, (4) Extension Area and (5) TGA File Footer. The last 3 areas, the Developer Area, the Extension Area and the TGA File Footer are new to the file specification as of September, 1989. For this reason, images created with software written before September, 1989 will probably not contain these three fields.";i:20;s:4:"Text";i:21;N;i:22;a:3:{i:0;s:122:"https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/PRONOM/Format/proFormatSearch.aspx?status=detailReport&id=533&strPageToDisplay=summary";i:1;s:44:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Truevision_TGA";i:2;s:67:"http://www.dca.fee.unicamp.br/~martino/disciplinas/ea978/tgaffs.pdf";}}i:14;a:23:{i:0;i:0;i:1;i:2472;i:2;s:2:"sr";i:3;s:341:"[\x00-\xff]{12,128}\x4f\x53\x2f\x32[\x00-\xff]{0,256}\x63\x6d\x61\x70[\x00-\xff]{0,256}\x67\x6c\x79\x66[\x00-\xff]{0,256}\x68\x65\x61\x64[\x00-\xff]{0,256}\x68\x68\x65\x61[\x00-\xff]{0,256}\x68\x6d\x74\x78[\x00-\xff]{0,256}\x6c\x6f\x63\x61[\x00-\xff]{0,256}\x6d\x61\x78\x70[\x00-\xff]{0,256}\x6e\x61\x6d\x65[\x00-\xff]{0,256}\x70\x6f\x73\x74";i:4;i:0;i:5;i:0;i:6;s:1:"s";i:7;s:0:"";i:8;s:3:"ttf";i:9;a:1:{i:0;s:3:"ttf";}i:10;s:8:"font/ttf";i:11;s:24:"public.truetype-ttf-font";i:12;s:19:"TrueType Font (TTF)";i:13;N;i:14;N;i:15;s:9:"x-fmt/453";i:16;N;i:17;s:4:"Font";i:18;s:4:"Full";i:19;s:251:"TrueType is an outline font standard developed by Apple in the late 1980s as a competitor to Adobe's Type 1 fonts used in PostScript. It has become the most common format for fonts on the classic Mac OS, macOS, and Microsoft Windows operating systems.";i:20;s:6:"Binary";i:21;N;i:22;a:2:{i:0;s:122:"https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/PRONOM/Format/proFormatSearch.aspx?status=detailReport&id=869&strPageToDisplay=summary";i:1;s:38:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/TrueType";}}i:15;a:23:{i:0;i:0;i:1;i:128;i:2;s:2:"sr";i:3;s:99:"\x0a\x00\x01[\x01\x02\x04\x08][\x00-\xff]{60}\x00[\x00-\xff]{3}[\x01\x02]\x00[\x00-\xff]{4}\x00{54}";i:4;i:0;i:5;i:0;i:6;s:1:"s";i:7;s:0:"";i:8;s:3:"pcx";i:9;a:1:{i:0;s:3:"pcx";}i:10;s:20:"image/vnd.zbrush.pcx";i:11;N;i:12;s:29:"PiCture eXchange Format (PCX)";i:13;s:1:"0";i:14;s:30:"ZSoft PC Paintbrush Bitmap (0)";i:15;s:6:"fmt/86";i:16;N;i:17;s:14:"Image (Raster)";i:18;N;i:19;s:542:"PCX, standing for PiCture eXchange, is an image file format developed by the now-defunct ZSoft Corporation of Marietta, Georgia, United States. It was the native file format for PC Paintbrush and became one of the first widely accepted DOS imaging standards, although it has since been succeeded by more sophisticated image formats, such as BMP, JPEG, and PNG. PCX files commonly stored palette-indexed images ranging from 2 or 4 colors to 16 and 256 colors, although the format has been extended to record true-color (24-bit) images as well.";i:20;s:6:"Binary";i:21;s:21:"Little-endian (Intel)";i:22;a:4:{i:0;s:122:"https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/PRONOM/Format/proFormatSearch.aspx?status=detailReport&id=621&strPageToDisplay=summary";i:1;s:33:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/PCX";i:2;s:45:"http://www.shikadi.net/moddingwiki/PCX_Format";i:3;s:36:"http://bespin.org/~qz/pc-gpe/pcx.txt";}}i:16;a:23:{i:0;i:0;i:1;i:128;i:2;s:2:"sr";i:3;s:99:"\x0a\x02\x01[\x01\x02\x04\x08][\x00-\xff]{60}\x00[\x00-\xff]{3}[\x01\x02]\x00[\x00-\xff]{4}\x00{54}";i:4;i:0;i:5;i:0;i:6;s:1:"s";i:7;s:0:"";i:8;s:3:"pcx";i:9;a:1:{i:0;s:3:"pcx";}i:10;s:20:"image/vnd.zbrush.pcx";i:11;N;i:12;s:29:"PiCture eXchange Format (PCX)";i:13;s:1:"2";i:14;s:30:"ZSoft PC Paintbrush Bitmap (2)";i:15;s:6:"fmt/87";i:16;N;i:17;s:14:"Image (Raster)";i:18;N;i:19;s:542:"PCX, standing for PiCture eXchange, is an image file format developed by the now-defunct ZSoft Corporation of Marietta, Georgia, United States. It was the native file format for PC Paintbrush and became one of the first widely accepted DOS imaging standards, although it has since been succeeded by more sophisticated image formats, such as BMP, JPEG, and PNG. PCX files commonly stored palette-indexed images ranging from 2 or 4 colors to 16 and 256 colors, although the format has been extended to record true-color (24-bit) images as well.";i:20;s:6:"Binary";i:21;s:21:"Little-endian (Intel)";i:22;a:4:{i:0;s:122:"https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/PRONOM/Format/proFormatSearch.aspx?status=detailReport&id=622&strPageToDisplay=summary";i:1;s:33:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/PCX";i:2;s:45:"http://www.shikadi.net/moddingwiki/PCX_Format";i:3;s:36:"http://bespin.org/~qz/pc-gpe/pcx.txt";}}i:17;a:23:{i:0;i:0;i:1;i:128;i:2;s:2:"sr";i:3;s:99:"\x0a\x03\x01[\x01\x02\x04\x08][\x00-\xff]{60}\x00[\x00-\xff]{3}[\x01\x02]\x00[\x00-\xff]{4}\x00{54}";i:4;i:0;i:5;i:0;i:6;s:1:"s";i:7;s:0:"";i:8;s:3:"pcx";i:9;a:1:{i:0;s:3:"pcx";}i:10;s:20:"image/vnd.zbrush.pcx";i:11;N;i:12;s:29:"PiCture eXchange Format (PCX)";i:13;s:1:"3";i:14;s:30:"ZSoft PC Paintbrush Bitmap (3)";i:15;s:6:"fmt/88";i:16;N;i:17;s:14:"Image (Raster)";i:18;N;i:19;s:542:"PCX, standing for PiCture eXchange, is an image file format developed by the now-defunct ZSoft Corporation of Marietta, Georgia, United States. It was the native file format for PC Paintbrush and became one of the first widely accepted DOS imaging standards, although it has since been succeeded by more sophisticated image formats, such as BMP, JPEG, and PNG. PCX files commonly stored palette-indexed images ranging from 2 or 4 colors to 16 and 256 colors, although the format has been extended to record true-color (24-bit) images as well.";i:20;s:6:"Binary";i:21;s:21:"Little-endian (Intel)";i:22;a:4:{i:0;s:122:"https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/PRONOM/Format/proFormatSearch.aspx?status=detailReport&id=623&strPageToDisplay=summary";i:1;s:33:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/PCX";i:2;s:45:"http://www.shikadi.net/moddingwiki/PCX_Format";i:3;s:36:"http://bespin.org/~qz/pc-gpe/pcx.txt";}}i:18;a:23:{i:0;i:0;i:1;i:128;i:2;s:2:"sr";i:3;s:99:"\x0a\x04\x01[\x01\x02\x04\x08][\x00-\xff]{60}\x00[\x00-\xff]{3}[\x01\x02]\x00[\x00-\xff]{4}\x00{54}";i:4;i:0;i:5;i:0;i:6;s:1:"s";i:7;s:0:"";i:8;s:3:"pcx";i:9;a:1:{i:0;s:3:"pcx";}i:10;s:20:"image/vnd.zbrush.pcx";i:11;N;i:12;s:29:"PiCture eXchange Format (PCX)";i:13;s:1:"4";i:14;s:30:"ZSoft PC Paintbrush Bitmap (4)";i:15;s:6:"fmt/89";i:16;N;i:17;s:14:"Image (Raster)";i:18;N;i:19;s:542:"PCX, standing for PiCture eXchange, is an image file format developed by the now-defunct ZSoft Corporation of Marietta, Georgia, United States. It was the native file format for PC Paintbrush and became one of the first widely accepted DOS imaging standards, although it has since been succeeded by more sophisticated image formats, such as BMP, JPEG, and PNG. PCX files commonly stored palette-indexed images ranging from 2 or 4 colors to 16 and 256 colors, although the format has been extended to record true-color (24-bit) images as well.";i:20;s:6:"Binary";i:21;s:21:"Little-endian (Intel)";i:22;a:4:{i:0;s:122:"https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/PRONOM/Format/proFormatSearch.aspx?status=detailReport&id=624&strPageToDisplay=summary";i:1;s:33:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/PCX";i:2;s:45:"http://www.shikadi.net/moddingwiki/PCX_Format";i:3;s:36:"http://bespin.org/~qz/pc-gpe/pcx.txt";}}i:19;a:23:{i:0;i:0;i:1;i:128;i:2;s:2:"sr";i:3;s:116:"\x0a\x05\x01[\x01\x02\x04\x08][\x00-\xff]{60}[\x00\x20][\x00-\xff]{3}[\x00\x01\x02][\x00-\xff]{5}(\x00{54}|\x20{54})";i:4;i:0;i:5;i:0;i:6;s:1:"s";i:7;s:0:"";i:8;s:3:"pcx";i:9;a:1:{i:0;s:3:"pcx";}i:10;s:20:"image/vnd.zbrush.pcx";i:11;N;i:12;s:29:"PiCture eXchange Format (PCX)";i:13;s:1:"5";i:14;s:30:"ZSoft PC Paintbrush Bitmap (5)";i:15;s:6:"fmt/90";i:16;N;i:17;s:14:"Image (Raster)";i:18;N;i:19;s:542:"PCX, standing for PiCture eXchange, is an image file format developed by the now-defunct ZSoft Corporation of Marietta, Georgia, United States. It was the native file format for PC Paintbrush and became one of the first widely accepted DOS imaging standards, although it has since been succeeded by more sophisticated image formats, such as BMP, JPEG, and PNG. PCX files commonly stored palette-indexed images ranging from 2 or 4 colors to 16 and 256 colors, although the format has been extended to record true-color (24-bit) images as well.";i:20;s:6:"Binary";i:21;s:21:"Little-endian (Intel)";i:22;a:4:{i:0;s:122:"https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/PRONOM/Format/proFormatSearch.aspx?status=detailReport&id=625&strPageToDisplay=summary";i:1;s:33:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/PCX";i:2;s:45:"http://www.shikadi.net/moddingwiki/PCX_Format";i:3;s:36:"http://bespin.org/~qz/pc-gpe/pcx.txt";}}i:20;a:23:{i:0;i:0;i:1;i:83;i:2;s:2:"sr";i:3;s:89:"\x50\x35[\x20\x0d\x0a][\x00-\xff]{0,2}\x23[\x00-\xff]{0,70}\x0a[\x30-\x39\x20\x0a\x0d]{6}";i:4;i:0;i:5;i:0;i:6;s:1:"s";i:7;s:0:"";i:8;s:3:"pgm";i:9;a:2:{i:0;s:3:"pgm";i:1;s:4:"pgmb";}i:10;s:24:"image/x-portable-graymap";i:11;N;i:12;s:39:"Portable Grey Map - Binary with comment";i:13;N;i:14;N;i:15;s:7:"fmt/406";i:16;N;i:17;N;i:18;N;i:19;s:400:"Several graphics formats are used and defined by the Netpbm project. The portable pixmap format (PPM), the portable graymap format (PGM) and the portable bitmap format (PBM) are image file formats designed to be easily exchanged between platforms. They are also sometimes referred to collectively as the portable anymap format (PNM), not to be confused with the related portable arbitrary map format.";i:20;s:6:"Binary";i:21;N;i:22;a:2:{i:0;s:123:"https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/PRONOM/Format/proFormatSearch.aspx?status=detailReport&id=1154&strPageToDisplay=summary";i:1;s:36:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Netpbm";}}i:21;a:23:{i:0;i:0;i:1;i:83;i:2;s:2:"sr";i:3;s:89:"\x50\x36[\x20\x0d\x0a][\x00-\xff]{0,2}\x23[\x00-\xff]{0,70}\x0a[\x30-\x39\x20\x0a\x0d]{6}";i:4;i:0;i:5;i:0;i:6;s:1:"s";i:7;s:0:"";i:8;s:3:"ppm";i:9;a:2:{i:0;s:3:"ppm";i:1;s:4:"ppmb";}i:10;s:23:"image/x-portable-pixmap";i:11;N;i:12;s:40:"Portable Pixel Map - Binary with comment";i:13;N;i:14;N;i:15;s:7:"fmt/408";i:16;N;i:17;N;i:18;N;i:19;s:400:"Several graphics formats are used and defined by the Netpbm project. The portable pixmap format (PPM), the portable graymap format (PGM) and the portable bitmap format (PBM) are image file formats designed to be easily exchanged between platforms. They are also sometimes referred to collectively as the portable anymap format (PNM), not to be confused with the related portable arbitrary map format.";i:20;s:6:"Binary";i:21;N;i:22;a:2:{i:0;s:123:"https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/PRONOM/Format/proFormatSearch.aspx?status=detailReport&id=1156&strPageToDisplay=summary";i:1;s:36:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Netpbm";}}i:22;a:23:{i:0;i:0;i:1;i:81;i:2;s:2:"sr";i:3;s:89:"\x50\x34[\x20\x0d\x0a][\x00-\xff]{0,2}\x23[\x00-\xff]{0,70}\x0a[\x30-\x39\x20\x0a\x0d]{4}";i:4;i:0;i:5;i:0;i:6;s:1:"s";i:7;s:0:"";i:8;s:3:"pbm";i:9;a:2:{i:0;s:3:"pbm";i:1;s:4:"pbmb";}i:10;s:23:"image/x-portable-bitmap";i:11;N;i:12;s:43:"Portable Bitmap Image - Binary with comment";i:13;N;i:14;N;i:15;s:7:"fmt/409";i:16;N;i:17;N;i:18;N;i:19;s:400:"Several graphics formats are used and defined by the Netpbm project. The portable pixmap format (PPM), the portable graymap format (PGM) and the portable bitmap format (PBM) are image file formats designed to be easily exchanged between platforms. They are also sometimes referred to collectively as the portable anymap format (PNM), not to be confused with the related portable arbitrary map format.";i:20;s:6:"Binary";i:21;N;i:22;a:2:{i:0;s:123:"https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/PRONOM/Format/proFormatSearch.aspx?status=detailReport&id=1157&strPageToDisplay=summary";i:1;s:36:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Netpbm";}}i:23;a:23:{i:0;i:0;i:1;i:58;i:2;s:2:"sr";i:3;s:143:"\x50\x4b\x03\x04[\x00-\xff]{26}\x6d\x69\x6d\x65\x74\x79\x70\x65\x61\x70\x70\x6c\x69\x63\x61\x74\x69\x6f\x6e\x2f\x65\x70\x75\x62\x2b\x7a\x69\x70";i:4;i:0;i:5;i:0;i:6;s:1:"s";i:7;s:0:"";i:8;s:4:"epub";i:9;a:1:{i:0;s:4:"epub";}i:10;s:20:"application/epub+zip";i:11;N;i:12;s:11:"ePub format";i:13;N;i:14;N;i:15;s:7:"fmt/483";i:16;N;i:17;s:17:"Text (Structured)";i:18;N;i:19;s:460:"EPUB is an e-book file format that uses the ".epub" file extension. The term is short for electronic publication and is sometimes styled ePub. EPUB is supported by many e-readers, and compatible software is available for most smartphones, tablets, and computers. EPUB is a technical standard published by the International Digital Publishing Forum (IDPF). It became an official standard of the IDPF in September 2007, superseding the older Open eBook standard.";i:20;N;i:21;N;i:22;a:2:{i:0;s:123:"https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/PRONOM/Format/proFormatSearch.aspx?status=detailReport&id=1270&strPageToDisplay=summary";i:1;s:34:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/EPUB";}}i:24;a:23:{i:0;i:0;i:1;i:48;i:2;s:2:"sr";i:3;s:49:"\x4f\x54\x54\x4f[\x00-\xff]{8,40}\x43\x46\x46\x20";i:4;i:0;i:5;i:0;i:6;s:1:"s";i:7;s:0:"";i:8;s:3:"otf";i:9;a:4:{i:0;s:3:"otf";i:1;s:3:"otc";i:2;s:3:"ttf";i:3;s:3:"ttc";}i:10;s:8:"font/otf";i:11;s:20:"public.opentype-font";i:12;s:18:"OpenType Font File";i:13;N;i:14;N;i:15;s:7:"fmt/520";i:16;N;i:17;s:4:"Font";i:18;N;i:19;s:269:"OpenType is a format for scalable computer fonts. It was built on its predecessor TrueType, retaining TrueType's basic structure and adding many intricate data structures for prescribing typographic behavior. OpenType is a registered trademark of Microsoft Corporation.";i:20;N;i:21;N;i:22;a:3:{i:0;s:123:"https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/PRONOM/Format/proFormatSearch.aspx?status=detailReport&id=1307&strPageToDisplay=summary";i:1;s:38:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/OpenType";i:2;s:58:"https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/typography/opentype/spec/";}}i:25;a:23:{i:0;i:0;i:1;i:45;i:2;s:2:"sr";i:3;s:65:"\x52\x61\x72\x21\x1a\x07\x00[\x00-\xff]{2}\x73[\x00-\xff]{34}\x14";i:4;i:0;i:5;i:0;i:6;s:1:"s";i:7;s:0:"";i:8;s:3:"rar";i:9;a:1:{i:0;s:3:"rar";}i:10;a:2:{i:0;s:28:"application/x-rar-compressed";i:1;s:19:"application/vnd.rar";}i:11;s:22:"com.rarlab.rar-archive";i:12;s:11:"RAR Archive";i:13;s:3:"2.0";i:14;N;i:15;s:9:"x-fmt/264";i:16;N;i:17;N;i:18;N;i:19;s:106:"RAR is a proprietary archive file format that supports data compression, error recovery and file spanning.";i:20;s:4:"Text";i:21;N;i:22;a:2:{i:0;s:122:"https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/PRONOM/Format/proFormatSearch.aspx?status=detailReport&id=384&strPageToDisplay=summary";i:1;s:47:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/RAR_(file_format)";}}i:26;a:23:{i:0;i:0;i:1;i:45;i:2;s:2:"sr";i:3;s:65:"\x52\x61\x72\x21\x1a\x07\x00[\x00-\xff]{2}\x73[\x00-\xff]{34}\x1d";i:4;i:0;i:5;i:0;i:6;s:1:"s";i:7;s:0:"";i:8;s:3:"rar";i:9;a:1:{i:0;s:3:"rar";}i:10;a:2:{i:0;s:28:"application/x-rar-compressed";i:1;s:19:"application/vnd.rar";}i:11;s:22:"com.rarlab.rar-archive";i:12;s:11:"RAR Archive";i:13;s:3:"2.9";i:14;N;i:15;s:7:"fmt/411";i:16;N;i:17;N;i:18;N;i:19;s:106:"RAR is a proprietary archive file format that supports data compression, error recovery and file spanning.";i:20;N;i:21;N;i:22;a:2:{i:0;s:123:"https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/PRONOM/Format/proFormatSearch.aspx?status=detailReport&id=1159&strPageToDisplay=summary";i:1;s:47:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/RAR_(file_format)";}}i:27;a:23:{i:0;i:0;i:1;i:41;i:2;s:2:"sr";i:3;s:120:"(?:\x2e\x73\x6e\x64|\x64\x6e\x73\x2e)[\x00-\xff]{20}\x41\x75\x64\x61\x63\x69\x74\x79\x42\x6c\x6f\x63\x6b\x46\x69\x6c\x65";i:4;i:0;i:5;i:0;i:6;s:1:"s";i:7;s:0:"";i:8;s:2:"au";i:9;a:1:{i:0;s:2:"au";}i:10;N;i:11;N;i:12;s:25:"Audacity Audio Block File";i:13;N;i:14;N;i:15;s:8:"fmt/1822";i:16;N;i:17;s:5:"Audio";i:18;N;i:19;s:550:"Audacity is free and open source audio editing, recording and post-processing software. The first version (0.8) was released in 2000 by Dominic Mazzoni and Roger Dannenberg at Carnegie Mellon University, Pennsylvania. It remains available today. An Audacity project consists of an aup project file, which is the name of the project followed by .aup . Next to the .aup file is a folder containing many .au files, each one being a segment of audio data. The aup file describes how audacity links the au files together to form the tracks in the project.";i:20;N;i:21;N;i:22;a:1:{i:0;s:123:"https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/PRONOM/Format/proFormatSearch.aspx?status=detailReport&id=2673&strPageToDisplay=summary";}}i:28;a:23:{i:0;i:0;i:1;i:38;i:2;s:2:"sr";i:3;s:115:"\xff\xd8\xff\xe8\x00\x20\x53\x50\x49\x46\x46\x00\x01\x00[\x00-\x04][\x00-\xff]{11}[\x00-\x05][\x00-\xff]{9}\xff\xe8";i:4;i:0;i:5;i:0;i:6;s:1:"s";i:7;s:0:"";i:8;s:3:"jpg";i:9;a:5:{i:0;s:3:"jpe";i:1;s:4:"jpeg";i:2;s:3:"jpg";i:3;s:3:"spf";i:4;s:5:"spiff";}i:10;s:10:"image/jpeg";i:11;s:11:"public.jpeg";i:12;s:37:"Still Picture Interchange File Format";i:13;s:3:"1.0";i:14;s:11:"SPIFF (1.0)";i:15;s:7:"fmt/112";i:16;N;i:17;s:14:"Image (Raster)";i:18;N;i:19;s:990:"The JPEG File Interchange Format (JFIF) is a file format for storing JPEG-compressed raster images. It was developed by the Independent JPEG Group and C-Cube Microsystems, in the absence of any such format being defined in the JPEG standard, and rapidly became a de facto standard; this is what is commonly referred to as the JPEG file format. A JFIF file comprises a JPEG data stream together with a JFIF marker. It begins with a Start of Image (SOI) marker, immediately followed by a JFIF Application (APP0). This is followed by the JPEG image data, which is terminated by an End of Image (EOI) marker. JFIF supports up to 24-bit colour and uses lossy compression (based on the Discrete Cosine Transform algorithm). Other types of compression are available through JPEG extensions, including progressive image buildup, arithmetic encoding, variable quantization, selective refinement, image tiling, and lossless compression, but these may not be supported by all JFIF readers and writers.";i:20;s:6:"Binary";i:21;s:21:"Big-endian (Motorola)";i:22;a:5:{i:0;s:122:"https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/PRONOM/Format/proFormatSearch.aspx?status=detailReport&id=671&strPageToDisplay=summary";i:1;s:68:"https://www.loc.gov/preservation/digital/formats/fdd/fdd000019.shtml";i:2;s:58:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/JPEG_File_Interchange_Format";i:3;s:48:"http://www.fileformat.info/format/spiff/egff.htm";i:4;s:85:"https://web.archive.org/web/20111218193625if_/http://www.jpeg.org:80/public/spiff.pdf";}}i:29;a:23:{i:0;i:0;i:1;i:35;i:2;s:2:"sr";i:3;s:154:"\x42\x4d[\x00-\xff]{4}\x00\x00\x00\x00[\x00-\xff]{4}\x6c\x00\x00\x00[\x00-\xff]{8}\x01\x00[\x01\x04\x08\x10\x18\x20]\x00[\x00\x01\x02\x03]\x00\x00\x00\x00";i:4;i:0;i:5;i:0;i:6;s:1:"s";i:7;s:0:"";i:8;s:3:"bmp";i:9;a:2:{i:0;s:3:"bmp";i:1;s:3:"dib";}i:10;s:9:"image/bmp";i:11;s:17:"com.microsoft.bmp";i:12;s:14:"Windows Bitmap";i:13;s:3:"4.0";i:14;s:9:"BMP (4.0)";i:15;s:7:"fmt/118";i:16;N;i:17;s:14:"Image (Raster)";i:18;N;i:19;s:311:"The BMP file format, also known as bitmap image file, device independent bitmap (DIB) file format and bitmap, is a raster graphics image file format used to store bitmap digital images, independently of the display device (such as a graphics adapter), especially on Microsoft Windows and OS/2 operating systems.";i:20;s:6:"Binary";i:21;s:21:"Little-endian (Intel)";i:22;a:3:{i:0;s:122:"https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/PRONOM/Format/proFormatSearch.aspx?status=detailReport&id=731&strPageToDisplay=summary";i:1;s:45:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/BMP_file_format";i:2;s:93:"https://medium.com/sysf/bits-to-bitmaps-a-simple-walkthrough-of-bmp-image-format-765dc6857393";}}i:30;a:23:{i:0;i:0;i:1;i:35;i:2;s:2:"sr";i:3;s:162:"\x42\x4d[\x00-\xff]{4}\x00\x00\x00\x00[\x00-\xff]{4}\x7c\x00\x00\x00[\x00-\xff]{8}\x01\x00[\x01\x04\x08\x10\x18\x20]\x00[\x00\x01\x02\x03\x04\x05]\x00\x00\x00\x00";i:4;i:0;i:5;i:0;i:6;s:1:"s";i:7;s:0:"";i:8;s:3:"bmp";i:9;a:2:{i:0;s:3:"bmp";i:1;s:3:"dib";}i:10;s:9:"image/bmp";i:11;s:17:"com.microsoft.bmp";i:12;s:14:"Windows Bitmap";i:13;s:3:"5.0";i:14;s:9:"BMP (5.0)";i:15;s:7:"fmt/119";i:16;N;i:17;s:14:"Image (Raster)";i:18;N;i:19;s:311:"The BMP file format, also known as bitmap image file, device independent bitmap (DIB) file format and bitmap, is a raster graphics image file format used to store bitmap digital images, independently of the display device (such as a graphics adapter), especially on Microsoft Windows and OS/2 operating systems.";i:20;s:6:"Binary";i:21;s:21:"Little-endian (Intel)";i:22;a:3:{i:0;s:122:"https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/PRONOM/Format/proFormatSearch.aspx?status=detailReport&id=732&strPageToDisplay=summary";i:1;s:45:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/BMP_file_format";i:2;s:93:"https://medium.com/sysf/bits-to-bitmaps-a-simple-walkthrough-of-bmp-image-format-765dc6857393";}}i:31;a:23:{i:0;i:0;i:1;i:34;i:2;s:2:"sr";i:3;s:142:"\x42\x4d[\x00-\xff]{4}\x00\x00\x00\x00[\x00-\xff]{4}\x28\x00\x00\x00[\x00-\xff]{8}\x01\x00[\x01\x04\x08\x18\x20]\x00[\x00\x01\x02]\x00\x00\x00";i:4;i:0;i:5;i:0;i:6;s:1:"s";i:7;s:0:"";i:8;s:3:"bmp";i:9;a:2:{i:0;s:3:"bmp";i:1;s:3:"dib";}i:10;s:9:"image/bmp";i:11;s:17:"com.microsoft.bmp";i:12;s:14:"Windows Bitmap";i:13;s:3:"3.0";i:14;s:9:"BMP (3.0)";i:15;s:7:"fmt/116";i:16;N;i:17;s:14:"Image (Raster)";i:18;N;i:19;s:311:"The BMP file format, also known as bitmap image file, device independent bitmap (DIB) file format and bitmap, is a raster graphics image file format used to store bitmap digital images, independently of the display device (such as a graphics adapter), especially on Microsoft Windows and OS/2 operating systems.";i:20;s:6:"Binary";i:21;s:21:"Little-endian (Intel)";i:22;a:3:{i:0;s:122:"https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/PRONOM/Format/proFormatSearch.aspx?status=detailReport&id=729&strPageToDisplay=summary";i:1;s:45:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/BMP_file_format";i:2;s:93:"https://medium.com/sysf/bits-to-bitmaps-a-simple-walkthrough-of-bmp-image-format-765dc6857393";}}i:32;a:23:{i:0;i:0;i:1;i:34;i:2;s:2:"sr";i:3;s:120:"\x42\x4d[\x00-\xff]{4}\x00\x00\x00\x00[\x00-\xff]{4}\x28\x00\x00\x00[\x00-\xff]{8}\x01\x00[\x10\x20]\x00\x03\x00\x00\x00";i:4;i:0;i:5;i:0;i:6;s:1:"s";i:7;s:0:"";i:8;s:3:"bmp";i:9;a:2:{i:0;s:3:"bmp";i:1;s:3:"dib";}i:10;s:9:"image/bmp";i:11;s:17:"com.microsoft.bmp";i:12;s:14:"Windows Bitmap";i:13;s:6:"3.0 NT";i:14;s:12:"BMP (3.0 NT)";i:15;s:7:"fmt/117";i:16;N;i:17;s:14:"Image (Raster)";i:18;N;i:19;s:311:"The BMP file format, also known as bitmap image file, device independent bitmap (DIB) file format and bitmap, is a raster graphics image file format used to store bitmap digital images, independently of the display device (such as a graphics adapter), especially on Microsoft Windows and OS/2 operating systems.";i:20;s:6:"Binary";i:21;s:21:"Little-endian (Intel)";i:22;a:3:{i:0;s:122:"https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/PRONOM/Format/proFormatSearch.aspx?status=detailReport&id=730&strPageToDisplay=summary";i:1;s:45:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/BMP_file_format";i:2;s:93:"https://medium.com/sysf/bits-to-bitmaps-a-simple-walkthrough-of-bmp-image-format-765dc6857393";}}i:33;a:23:{i:0;i:0;i:1;i:34;i:2;s:2:"sr";i:3;s:85:"\x42\x4d[\x00-\xff]{12}\x28\x00\x00\x00[\x00-\xff]{8}\x01\x00\x18\x00\x04\x00\x00\x00";i:4;i:0;i:5;i:0;i:6;s:1:"s";i:7;s:0:"";i:8;s:3:"bmp";i:9;a:1:{i:0;s:3:"bmp";}i:10;s:9:"image/bmp";i:11;N;i:12;s:11:"OS/2 Bitmap";i:13;s:3:"2.0";i:14;N;i:15;s:9:"x-fmt/270";i:16;N;i:17;s:14:"Image (Raster)";i:18;N;i:19;s:438:"The IBM OS/2 Bitmap (BMP) file format is one of several graphics file formats supported by the OS/2 operating system. BMP is the native bitmap format of OS/2 and is used to store several types of bitmap data, including icons and pointers. Most graphics and imaging applications operating under OS/2 support the creation and display of BMP format files. BMP is also found in MS-DOS and Microsoft Windows and originated in that environment.";i:20;s:4:"Text";i:21;N;i:22;a:3:{i:0;s:122:"https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/PRONOM/Format/proFormatSearch.aspx?status=detailReport&id=402&strPageToDisplay=summary";i:1;s:45:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/BMP_file_format";i:2;s:50:"https://www.fileformat.info/format/os2bmp/egff.htm";}}i:34;a:23:{i:0;i:0;i:1;i:34;i:2;s:2:"sr";i:3;s:85:"\x42\x4d[\x00-\xff]{12}\x28\x00\x00\x00[\x00-\xff]{8}\x01\x00\x01\x00\x03\x00\x00\x00";i:4;i:0;i:5;i:0;i:6;s:1:"s";i:7;s:0:"";i:8;s:3:"bmp";i:9;a:1:{i:0;s:3:"bmp";}i:10;s:9:"image/bmp";i:11;N;i:12;s:11:"OS/2 Bitmap";i:13;s:3:"2.0";i:14;N;i:15;s:9:"x-fmt/270";i:16;N;i:17;s:14:"Image (Raster)";i:18;N;i:19;s:438:"The IBM OS/2 Bitmap (BMP) file format is one of several graphics file formats supported by the OS/2 operating system. BMP is the native bitmap format of OS/2 and is used to store several types of bitmap data, including icons and pointers. Most graphics and imaging applications operating under OS/2 support the creation and display of BMP format files. BMP is also found in MS-DOS and Microsoft Windows and originated in that environment.";i:20;s:4:"Text";i:21;N;i:22;a:3:{i:0;s:122:"https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/PRONOM/Format/proFormatSearch.aspx?status=detailReport&id=402&strPageToDisplay=summary";i:1;s:45:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/BMP_file_format";i:2;s:50:"https://www.fileformat.info/format/os2bmp/egff.htm";}}i:35;a:23:{i:0;i:0;i:1;i:28;i:2;s:2:"sr";i:3;s:71:"\x42\x4d[\x00-\xff]{12}[\x10\x30\x40]\x00\x00\x00[\x00-\xff]{8}\x01\x00";i:4;i:0;i:5;i:0;i:6;s:1:"s";i:7;s:0:"";i:8;s:3:"bmp";i:9;a:1:{i:0;s:3:"bmp";}i:10;s:9:"image/bmp";i:11;N;i:12;s:11:"OS/2 Bitmap";i:13;s:3:"2.0";i:14;N;i:15;s:9:"x-fmt/270";i:16;N;i:17;s:14:"Image (Raster)";i:18;N;i:19;s:438:"The IBM OS/2 Bitmap (BMP) file format is one of several graphics file formats supported by the OS/2 operating system. BMP is the native bitmap format of OS/2 and is used to store several types of bitmap data, including icons and pointers. Most graphics and imaging applications operating under OS/2 support the creation and display of BMP format files. BMP is also found in MS-DOS and Microsoft Windows and originated in that environment.";i:20;s:4:"Text";i:21;N;i:22;a:3:{i:0;s:122:"https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/PRONOM/Format/proFormatSearch.aspx?status=detailReport&id=402&strPageToDisplay=summary";i:1;s:45:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/BMP_file_format";i:2;s:50:"https://www.fileformat.info/format/os2bmp/egff.htm";}}i:36;a:23:{i:0;i:0;i:1;i:26;i:2;s:2:"sr";i:3;s:112:"\x42\x4d[\x00-\xff]{4}\x00\x00\x00\x00[\x00-\xff]{4}\x0c\x00\x00\x00[\x00-\xff]{4}\x01\x00[\x01\x04\x08\x18]\x00";i:4;i:0;i:5;i:0;i:6;s:1:"s";i:7;s:0:"";i:8;s:3:"bmp";i:9;a:2:{i:0;s:3:"bmp";i:1;s:3:"dib";}i:10;s:9:"image/bmp";i:11;s:17:"com.microsoft.bmp";i:12;s:14:"Windows Bitmap";i:13;s:3:"2.0";i:14;s:9:"BMP (2.0)";i:15;s:7:"fmt/115";i:16;N;i:17;s:14:"Image (Raster)";i:18;N;i:19;s:311:"The BMP file format, also known as bitmap image file, device independent bitmap (DIB) file format and bitmap, is a raster graphics image file format used to store bitmap digital images, independently of the display device (such as a graphics adapter), especially on Microsoft Windows and OS/2 operating systems.";i:20;s:6:"Binary";i:21;s:21:"Little-endian (Intel)";i:22;a:3:{i:0;s:122:"https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/PRONOM/Format/proFormatSearch.aspx?status=detailReport&id=728&strPageToDisplay=summary";i:1;s:45:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/BMP_file_format";i:2;s:93:"https://medium.com/sysf/bits-to-bitmaps-a-simple-walkthrough-of-bmp-image-format-765dc6857393";}}i:37;a:23:{i:0;i:0;i:1;i:26;i:2;s:2:"sr";i:3;s:39:"\x4d\x53\x43\x46[\x00-\xff]{20}\x03\x01";i:4;i:0;i:5;i:0;i:6;s:1:"s";i:7;s:0:"";i:8;s:3:"cab";i:9;a:1:{i:0;s:3:"cab";}i:10;s:33:"application/vnd.ms-cab-compressed";i:11;s:18:"public.archive.cab";i:12;s:20:"Windows Cabinet File";i:13;N;i:14;N;i:15;s:9:"x-fmt/414";i:16;N;i:17;N;i:18;N;i:19;s:324:"Cabinet (or CAB) is an archive-file format for Microsoft Windows that supports lossless data compression and embedded digital certificates used for maintaining archive integrity. Cabinet files have .cab filename extensions and are recognized by their first 4 bytes MSCF. Cabinet files were known originally as Diamond files.";i:20;s:4:"Text";i:21;N;i:22;a:2:{i:0;s:122:"https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/PRONOM/Format/proFormatSearch.aspx?status=detailReport&id=801&strPageToDisplay=summary";i:1;s:51:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cabinet_(file_format)";}}i:38;a:23:{i:0;i:0;i:1;i:25;i:2;s:2:"sr";i:3;s:121:"\x25\x21\x50\x53\x2d\x41\x64\x6f\x62\x65\x2d\x33\x2e\x30\x20\x45\x50\x53\x46\x2d\x33\x2e\x30(\x0d|\x0a|\x0d\x0a|\x0a\x0d)";i:4;i:0;i:5;i:0;i:6;s:1:"s";i:7;s:0:"";i:8;s:3:"eps";i:9;a:3:{i:0;s:3:"eps";i:1;s:4:"epsf";i:2;s:2:"ps";}i:10;s:22:"application/postscript";i:11;s:33:"com.adobe.encapsulated-postscript";i:12;s:35:"Encapsulated PostScript File Format";i:13;s:1:"3";i:14;s:9:"EPS (3.0)";i:15;s:7:"fmt/124";i:16;N;i:17;s:16:"Page Description";i:18;s:4:"Full";i:19;s:539:"Encapsulated Postscript (EPS) is a page description format, developed by Adobe Systems Incorporated. Version 3, the third version of the format, was developed in 1992, and subsequently revised in 1999 to include a number of extensions and features of the PostScript language not previously documented. An EPS file comprises a header section followed by a PostScript document. The header can refer to version 3.0 or version 3.1. An EPS can optionally include a preview raster image, in which case a binary header is prepended to the header.";i:20;s:4:"Text";i:21;N;i:22;a:5:{i:0;s:122:"https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/PRONOM/Format/proFormatSearch.aspx?status=detailReport&id=331&strPageToDisplay=summary";i:1;s:53:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Encapsulated_PostScript";i:2;s:135:"https://web.archive.org/web/20170818010030if_/http://wwwimages.adobe.com/content/dam/Adobe/en/devnet/postscript/pdfs/5002.EPSF_Spec.pdf";i:3;s:31:"http://www.mjr19.org.uk/eps.pdf";i:4;s:20:"https://epsfile.top/";}}i:39;a:23:{i:0;i:0;i:1;i:25;i:2;s:2:"sr";i:3;s:121:"\x25\x21\x50\x53\x2d\x41\x64\x6f\x62\x65\x2d\x33\x2e\x31\x20\x45\x50\x53\x46\x2d\x33\x2e\x30(\x0d|\x0a|\x0d\x0a|\x0a\x0d)";i:4;i:0;i:5;i:0;i:6;s:1:"s";i:7;s:0:"";i:8;s:3:"eps";i:9;a:3:{i:0;s:3:"eps";i:1;s:4:"epsf";i:2;s:2:"ps";}i:10;s:22:"application/postscript";i:11;s:33:"com.adobe.encapsulated-postscript";i:12;s:35:"Encapsulated PostScript File Format";i:13;s:3:"3.1";i:14;s:9:"EPS (3.1)";i:15;s:7:"fmt/124";i:16;N;i:17;s:16:"Page Description";i:18;s:4:"Full";i:19;s:539:"Encapsulated Postscript (EPS) is a page description format, developed by Adobe Systems Incorporated. Version 3, the third version of the format, was developed in 1992, and subsequently revised in 1999 to include a number of extensions and features of the PostScript language not previously documented. An EPS file comprises a header section followed by a PostScript document. The header can refer to version 3.0 or version 3.1. An EPS can optionally include a preview raster image, in which case a binary header is prepended to the header.";i:20;s:4:"Text";i:21;N;i:22;a:5:{i:0;s:122:"https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/PRONOM/Format/proFormatSearch.aspx?status=detailReport&id=331&strPageToDisplay=summary";i:1;s:53:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Encapsulated_PostScript";i:2;s:135:"https://web.archive.org/web/20170818010030if_/http://wwwimages.adobe.com/content/dam/Adobe/en/devnet/postscript/pdfs/5002.EPSF_Spec.pdf";i:3;s:31:"http://www.mjr19.org.uk/eps.pdf";i:4;s:20:"https://epsfile.top/";}}i:40;a:23:{i:0;i:0;i:1;i:24;i:2;s:2:"sr";i:3;s:111:"\x2e\x73\x6e\x64\x00\x00\x00[\x00-\xff]{5}\x00\x00\x00[\x01-\x07\x17-\x1b][\x00-\xff]{4}\x00\x00\x00[\x00-\xff]";i:4;i:0;i:5;i:0;i:6;s:1:"s";i:7;s:0:"";i:8;s:2:"au";i:9;a:2:{i:0;s:2:"au";i:1;s:3:"snd";}i:10;s:11:"audio/basic";i:11;N;i:12;s:14:"NeXT/Sun sound";i:13;N;i:14;N;i:15;s:9:"x-fmt/139";i:16;N;i:17;s:5:"Audio";i:18;N;i:19;s:399:"This is an outline record only, and requires further details, research or authentication to provide information that will enable users to further understand the format and to assess digital preservation risks associated with it if appropriate. If you are able to help by supplying any additional information concerning this entry, please return to the main PRONOM page and select â€˜Add an Entryâ€™.";i:20;s:4:"Text";i:21;N;i:22;a:4:{i:0;s:122:"https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/PRONOM/Format/proFormatSearch.aspx?status=detailReport&id=199&strPageToDisplay=summary";i:1;s:53:"https://www.file-recovery.com/au-signature-format.htm";i:2;s:44:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Au_file_format";i:3;s:53:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_file_signatures";}}i:41;a:23:{i:0;i:0;i:1;i:24;i:2;s:2:"sr";i:3;s:94:"\x00\x00\x00\x0c\x6a\x50\x20\x20\x0d\x0a\x87\x0a[\x00-\xff]{4}\x66\x74\x79\x70\x6d\x6a\x70\x32";i:4;i:0;i:5;i:0;i:6;s:1:"s";i:7;s:0:"";i:8;s:3:"mj2";i:9;a:2:{i:0;s:3:"mj2";i:1;s:4:"mjp2";}i:10;s:9:"video/mj2";i:11;N;i:12;s:22:"MJ2 (Motion JPEG 2000)";i:13;N;i:14;N;i:15;s:7:"fmt/337";i:16;s:9:"JPEG 2000";i:17;s:5:"Video";i:18;N;i:19;s:242:"Motion JPEG 2000 (MJ2 or MJP2) is a file format for motion sequences of JPEG 2000 images and associated audio, based on the MP4/QuickTime format. Filename extensions for Motion JPEG 2000 video files are .mj2 and .mjp2, as defined in RFC 3745.";i:20;N;i:21;N;i:22;a:4:{i:0;s:123:"https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/PRONOM/Format/proFormatSearch.aspx?status=detailReport&id=1082&strPageToDisplay=summary";i:1;s:46:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Motion_JPEG_2000";i:2;s:49:"http://fileformats.archiveteam.org/wiki/JPEG_2000";i:3;s:26:"https://jpeg.org/jpeg2000/";}}i:42;a:23:{i:0;i:0;i:1;i:23;i:2;s:2:"sr";i:3;s:90:"\x00\x00\x00\x0c\x6a\x50\x20\x20\x0d\x0a\x87\x0a[\x00-\xff]{4}\x66\x74\x79\x70\x6a\x70\x32";i:4;i:0;i:5;i:0;i:6;s:1:"s";i:7;s:0:"";i:8;s:3:"jp2";i:9;a:2:{i:0;s:3:"jp2";i:1;s:3:"j2k";}i:10;s:9:"image/jp2";i:11;s:16:"public.jpeg-2000";i:12;s:22:"JP2 (JPEG 2000 part 1)";i:13;N;i:14;N;i:15;s:9:"x-fmt/392";i:16;s:9:"JPEG 2000";i:17;s:14:"Image (Raster)";i:18;N;i:19;s:618:"JPEG 2000 (JP2) is an image compression standard and coding system. It was developed from 1997 to 2000 by a Joint Photographic Experts Group committee chaired by Touradj Ebrahimi (later the JPEG president), with the intention of superseding their original discrete cosine transform (DCT) based JPEG standard (created in 1992) with a newly designed, wavelet-based method. The standardized filename extension is .jp2 for ISO/IEC 15444-1 conforming files and .jpx for the extended part-2 specifications, published as ISO/IEC 15444-2. The registered MIME types are defined in RFC 3745. For ISO/IEC 15444-1 it is image/jp2.";i:20;s:4:"Text";i:21;N;i:22;a:4:{i:0;s:122:"https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/PRONOM/Format/proFormatSearch.aspx?status=detailReport&id=686&strPageToDisplay=summary";i:1;s:39:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/JPEG_2000";i:2;s:49:"http://fileformats.archiveteam.org/wiki/JPEG_2000";i:3;s:26:"https://jpeg.org/jpeg2000/";}}i:43;a:23:{i:0;i:0;i:1;i:23;i:2;s:2:"sr";i:3;s:90:"\x00\x00\x00\x0c\x6a\x50\x20\x20\x0d\x0a\x87\x0a[\x00-\xff]{4}\x66\x74\x79\x70\x6a\x70\x6d";i:4;i:0;i:5;i:0;i:6;s:1:"s";i:7;s:0:"";i:8;s:3:"jpm";i:9;a:1:{i:0;s:3:"jpm";}i:10;s:9:"image/jpm";i:11;N;i:12;s:22:"JPM (JPEG 2000 part 6)";i:13;N;i:14;N;i:15;s:7:"fmt/463";i:16;s:9:"JPEG 2000";i:17;s:14:"Image (Raster)";i:18;N;i:19;s:324:"The JPEG 2000 Compound Image File Format is an optional file format for storing compound images using the JPEG 2000 file format family architecture. A compound image is an image that may contain scanned images, synthetic images or both, and that preferably requires a mix of continuous tone and bi-level compression methods.";i:20;N;i:21;N;i:22;a:4:{i:0;s:123:"https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/PRONOM/Format/proFormatSearch.aspx?status=detailReport&id=1250&strPageToDisplay=summary";i:1;s:39:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/JPEG_2000";i:2;s:49:"http://fileformats.archiveteam.org/wiki/JPEG_2000";i:3;s:26:"https://jpeg.org/jpeg2000/";}}i:44;a:23:{i:0;i:0;i:1;i:23;i:2;s:2:"sr";i:3;s:90:"\x00\x00\x00\x0c\x6a\x50\x20\x20\x0d\x0a\x87\x0a[\x00-\xff]{4}\x66\x74\x79\x70\x6a\x70\x78";i:4;i:0;i:5;i:0;i:6;s:1:"s";i:7;s:0:"";i:8;s:3:"jpx";i:9;a:2:{i:0;s:3:"jpx";i:1;s:3:"jpf";}i:10;s:9:"image/jpx";i:11;s:16:"public.jpeg-2000";i:12;s:22:"JPX (JPEG 2000 part 2)";i:13;N;i:14;s:3:"JPF";i:15;s:7:"fmt/151";i:16;s:9:"JPEG 2000";i:17;s:14:"Image (Raster)";i:18;N;i:19;s:292:"JPX is an image file format extended from JP2, providing the potential for extended colour space support, alternative compression methods, and other features. JPX may be used in applications that require additional functionality or data structures beyond those defined in the JP2 file format.";i:20;s:4:"Text";i:21;s:21:"Big-endian (Motorola)";i:22;a:4:{i:0;s:122:"https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/PRONOM/Format/proFormatSearch.aspx?status=detailReport&id=686&strPageToDisplay=summary";i:1;s:39:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/JPEG_2000";i:2;s:49:"http://fileformats.archiveteam.org/wiki/JPEG_2000";i:3;s:26:"https://jpeg.org/jpeg2000/";}}i:45;a:23:{i:0;i:0;i:1;i:20;i:2;s:2:"sr";i:3;s:115:"\x41\x54\x26\x54\x46\x4f\x52\x4d[\x00-\xff]{4}(?:\x44\x4A\x56\x4D\x44\x49\x52\x4D|\x44\x4A\x56\x55\x49\x4E\x46\x4F)";i:4;i:0;i:5;i:0;i:6;s:1:"s";i:7;s:0:"";i:8;s:3:"djv";i:9;a:2:{i:0;s:3:"djv";i:1;s:4:"djvu";}i:10;s:14:"image/vnd.djvu";i:11;N;i:12;s:16:"DjVu File Format";i:13;N;i:14;N;i:15;s:7:"fmt/255";i:16;N;i:17;s:14:"Image (Raster)";i:18;N;i:19;s:485:"DjVu is a computer file format designed primarily to store scanned documents, especially those containing a combination of text, line drawings, indexed color images, and photographs. It uses technologies such as image layer separation of text and background/images, progressive loading, arithmetic coding, and lossy compression for bitonal (monochrome) images. This allows high-quality, readable images to be stored in a minimum of space, so that they can be made available on the web.";i:20;s:4:"Text";i:21;N;i:22;a:2:{i:0;s:122:"https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/PRONOM/Format/proFormatSearch.aspx?status=detailReport&id=993&strPageToDisplay=summary";i:1;s:34:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/DjVu";}}i:46;a:23:{i:0;i:0;i:1;i:18;i:2;s:2:"sr";i:3;s:77:"\x4d\x54\x68\x64\x00\x00\x00\x06\x00[\x00-\x02][\x00-\xff]{4}\x4d\x54\x72\x6b";i:4;i:0;i:5;i:0;i:6;s:1:"s";i:7;s:0:"";i:8;s:3:"mid";i:9;a:3:{i:0;s:3:"kar";i:1;s:3:"mid";i:2;s:4:"midi";}i:10;s:10:"audio/midi";i:11;N;i:12;s:10:"MIDI Audio";i:13;N;i:14;N;i:15;s:7:"fmt/230";i:16;N;i:17;s:5:"Audio";i:18;N;i:19;s:536:"MIDI (an acronym for Musical Instrument Digital Interface) is a technical standard that describes a communications protocol, digital interface, and electrical connectors that connect a wide variety of electronic musical instruments, computers, and related audio devices for playing, editing and recording music. The specification originates in a paper published by Dave Smith and Chet Wood then of Sequential Circuits at the October 1981 Audio Engineering Society conference in New York City then titled Universal Synthesizer Interface.";i:20;s:4:"Text";i:21;N;i:22;a:3:{i:0;s:122:"https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/PRONOM/Format/proFormatSearch.aspx?status=detailReport&id=322&strPageToDisplay=summary";i:1;s:34:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/MIDI";i:2;s:68:"https://www.midi.org/specifications/item/the-midi-1-0-specification/";}}i:47;a:23:{i:0;i:0;i:1;i:18;i:2;s:1:"s";i:3;s:18:"TRUEVISION-XFILE.␀";i:4;i:0;i:5;i:0;i:6;s:1:"s";i:7;s:0:"";i:8;s:3:"tga";i:9;a:4:{i:0;s:3:"icb";i:1;s:3:"tga";i:2;s:3:"vda";i:3;s:3:"vst";}i:10;s:11:"image/x-tga";i:11;s:24:"com.truevision.tga-image";i:12;s:21:"Truevision TGA Bitmap";i:13;s:3:"2.0";i:14;N;i:15;s:7:"fmt/402";i:16;N;i:17;s:14:"Image (Raster)";i:18;N;i:19;s:514:"The Truevision TGA File Format comprises 5 areas, each of which contains one or more fields of fixed or variable length. The 5 file areas are: (1) TGA File Header, (2) Image/Color Map Data, (3) Developer Area, (4) Extension Area and (5) TGA File Footer. The last 3 areas, the Developer Area, the Extension Area and the TGA File Footer are new to the file specification as of September, 1989. For this reason, images created with software written before September, 1989 will probably not contain these three fields.";i:20;N;i:21;N;i:22;a:3:{i:0;s:123:"https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/PRONOM/Format/proFormatSearch.aspx?status=detailReport&id=1150&strPageToDisplay=summary";i:1;s:44:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Truevision_TGA";i:2;s:67:"http://www.dca.fee.unicamp.br/~martino/disciplinas/ea978/tgaffs.pdf";}}i:48;a:23:{i:0;i:0;i:1;i:16;i:2;s:1:"s";i:3;s:16:"‰PNG

␀␀␀IHDR";i:4;i:0;i:5;i:12;i:6;s:1:"s";i:7;s:12:"␀␀␀␀IEND®B`‚";i:8;s:3:"png";i:9;a:1:{i:0;s:3:"png";}i:10;s:9:"image/png";i:11;s:10:"public.png";i:12;s:31:"Portable Network Graphics (PNG)";i:13;N;i:14;N;i:15;N;i:16;N;i:17;s:14:"Image (Raster)";i:18;N;i:19;s:792:"Portable Network Graphics (PNG) is a raster-graphics file format that supports lossless data compression. PNG was developed as an improved, non-patented replacement for Graphics Interchange Format (GIF). PNG supports palette-based images (with palettes of 24-bit RGB or 32-bit RGBA colors), grayscale images (with or without alpha channel for transparency), and full-color non-palette-based RGB or RGBA images. The PNG working group designed the format for transferring images on the Internet, not for professional-quality print graphics; therefore non-RGB color spaces such as CMYK are not supported. A PNG file contains a single image in an extensible structure of chunks, encoding the basic pixels and other information such as textual comments and integrity checks documented in RFC 2083.";i:20;s:6:"Binary";i:21;s:21:"Big-endian (Motorola)";i:22;a:3:{i:0;s:55:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Portable_Network_Graphics";i:1;s:56:"http://www.libpng.org/pub/png/spec/1.2/PNG-Contents.html";i:2;s:44:"https://www.w3.org/TR/2003/REC-PNG-20031110/";}}i:49;a:23:{i:0;i:0;i:1;i:16;i:2;s:2:"sr";i:3;s:70:"\xff\xd8\xff\xe1[\x00-\xff]{2}\x45\x78\x69\x66\x00\x00\x4d\x4d\x00\x2a";i:4;i:0;i:5;i:2;i:6;s:1:"s";i:7;s:2:"ÿÙ";i:8;s:3:"jpg";i:9;a:3:{i:0;s:3:"jpe";i:1;s:4:"jpeg";i:2;s:3:"jpg";}i:10;s:10:"image/jpeg";i:11;s:11:"public.jpeg";i:12;s:43:"Exchangeable Image File Format (Compressed)";i:13;s:3:"2.0";i:14;s:27:"Exif Compressed Image (2.0)";i:15;s:9:"x-fmt/398";i:16;N;i:17;s:14:"Image (Raster)";i:18;N;i:19;s:252:"Exchangeable image file format (Exif) is a standard that specifies the formats for images, sound, and ancillary tags used by digital cameras (including smartphones), scanners and other systems handling image and sound files recorded by digital cameras.";i:20;s:6:"Binary";i:21;s:21:"Big-endian (Motorola)";i:22;a:2:{i:0;s:122:"https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/PRONOM/Format/proFormatSearch.aspx?status=detailReport&id=751&strPageToDisplay=summary";i:1;s:58:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/JPEG_File_Interchange_Format";}}i:50;a:23:{i:0;i:0;i:1;i:16;i:2;s:2:"sr";i:3;s:70:"\xff\xd8\xff\xe1[\x00-\xff]{2}\x45\x78\x69\x66\x00\x00\x49\x49\x2a\x00";i:4;i:0;i:5;i:2;i:6;s:1:"s";i:7;s:2:"ÿÙ";i:8;s:3:"jpg";i:9;a:3:{i:0;s:3:"jpe";i:1;s:4:"jpeg";i:2;s:3:"jpg";}i:10;s:10:"image/jpeg";i:11;s:11:"public.jpeg";i:12;s:43:"Exchangeable Image File Format (Compressed)";i:13;s:3:"2.0";i:14;s:27:"Exif Compressed Image (2.0)";i:15;s:9:"x-fmt/398";i:16;N;i:17;s:14:"Image (Raster)";i:18;N;i:19;s:252:"Exchangeable image file format (Exif) is a standard that specifies the formats for images, sound, and ancillary tags used by digital cameras (including smartphones), scanners and other systems handling image and sound files recorded by digital cameras.";i:20;s:6:"Binary";i:21;s:21:"Little-endian (Intel)";i:22;a:2:{i:0;s:122:"https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/PRONOM/Format/proFormatSearch.aspx?status=detailReport&id=751&strPageToDisplay=summary";i:1;s:58:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/JPEG_File_Interchange_Format";}}i:51;a:23:{i:0;i:0;i:1;i:16;i:2;s:2:"sr";i:3;s:70:"\xff\xd8\xff\xe1[\x00-\xff]{2}\x45\x78\x69\x66\x00\x00\x4d\x4d\x00\x2a";i:4;i:0;i:5;i:2;i:6;s:1:"s";i:7;s:2:"ÿÙ";i:8;s:3:"jpg";i:9;a:3:{i:0;s:3:"jpe";i:1;s:4:"jpeg";i:2;s:3:"jpg";}i:10;s:10:"image/jpeg";i:11;s:11:"public.jpeg";i:12;s:43:"Exchangeable Image File Format (Compressed)";i:13;s:3:"2.1";i:14;s:27:"Exif Compressed Image (2.1)";i:15;s:9:"x-fmt/390";i:16;N;i:17;s:14:"Image (Raster)";i:18;N;i:19;s:252:"Exchangeable image file format (Exif) is a standard that specifies the formats for images, sound, and ancillary tags used by digital cameras (including smartphones), scanners and other systems handling image and sound files recorded by digital cameras."