ce13.pdf";i:3;s:91:"https://www.adobe.com/content/dam/acom/en/devnet/pdf/pdf_reference_archive/PDFReference.pdf";i:4;s:96:"https://www.adobe.com/content/dam/acom/en/devnet/pdf/pdf_reference_archive/PDFReference15_v6.pdf";i:5;s:93:"https://www.adobe.com/content/dam/acom/en/devnet/pdf/pdf_reference_archive/PDFReference16.pdf";i:6;s:83:"https://www.adobe.com/content/dam/acom/en/devnet/acrobat/pdfs/pdf_reference_1-7.pdf";i:7;s:79:"https://www.adobe.com/content/dam/acom/en/devnet/acrobat/pdfs/PDF32000_2008.pdf";i:8;s:128:"https://web.archive.org/web/20130902000323if_/http://cdn.parleys.com/p/5148922a0364bc17fc56c6e5/iSUM2012_00_LRO_presentation.pdf";i:9;s:111:"https://web.archive.org/web/20180730100811if_/https://www.immagic.com/eLibrary/ARCHIVES/TECH/ADOBE/A070914X.pdf";i:10;s:71:"http://www.cs.nott.ac.uk/~psadb1/Publications/Download/2002/Hardy02.pdf";}}i:87;a:23:{i:0;i:0;i:1;i:8;i:2;s:1:"s";i:3;s:8:"%PDF-2.0";i:4;i:0;i:5;i:1024;i:6;s:2:"sr";i:7;s:39:"\x25\x25\x45\x4f\x46[\x00-\xff]{0,1019}";i:8;s:3:"pdf";i:9;a:1:{i:0;s:3:"pdf";}i:10;s:15:"application/pdf";i:11;s:13:"com.adobe.pdf";i:12;s:34:"PDF 2.0 - Portable Document Format";i:13;s:3:"2.0";i:14;N;i:15;s:8:"fmt/1129";i:16;N;i:17;s:16:"Page Description";i:18;N;i:19;s:543:"The Portable Document Format (PDF) is a file format developed by Adobe in 1993 to present documents, including text formatting and images, in a manner independent of application software, hardware, and operating systems. Based on the PostScript language, each PDF file encapsulates a complete description of a fixed-layout flat document, including the text, fonts, vector graphics, raster images and other information needed to display it. PDF was standardized as ISO 32000 in 2008, and no longer requires any royalties for its implementation.";i:20;N;i:21;N;i:22;a:11:{i:0;s:123:"https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/PRONOM/Format/proFormatSearch.aspx?status=detailReport&id=1939&strPageToDisplay=summary";i:1;s:33:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/PDF";i:2;s:76:"https://www.adobe.com/content/dam/acom/en/devnet/pdf/pdfs/PDFReference13.pdf";i:3;s:91:"https://www.adobe.com/content/dam/acom/en/devnet/pdf/pdf_reference_archive/PDFReference.pdf";i:4;s:96:"https://www.adobe.com/content/dam/acom/en/devnet/pdf/pdf_reference_archive/PDFReference15_v6.pdf";i:5;s:93:"https://www.adobe.com/content/dam/acom/en/devnet/pdf/pdf_reference_archive/PDFReference16.pdf";i:6;s:83:"https://www.adobe.com/content/dam/acom/en/devnet/acrobat/pdfs/pdf_reference_1-7.pdf";i:7;s:79:"https://www.adobe.com/content/dam/acom/en/devnet/acrobat/pdfs/PDF32000_2008.pdf";i:8;s:128:"https://web.archive.org/web/20130902000323if_/http://cdn.parleys.com/p/5148922a0364bc17fc56c6e5/iSUM2012_00_LRO_presentation.pdf";i:9;s:111:"https://web.archive.org/web/20180730100811if_/https://www.immagic.com/eLibrary/ARCHIVES/TECH/ADOBE/A070914X.pdf";i:10;s:71:"http://www.cs.nott.ac.uk/~psadb1/Publications/Download/2002/Hardy02.pdf";}}i:88;a:23:{i:0;i:0;i:1;i:8;i:2;s:1:"s";i:3;s:8:"fLaC␀␀␀"";i:4;i:0;i:5;i:0;i:6;s:1:"s";i:7;s:0:"";i:8;s:4:"flac";i:9;a:1:{i:0;s:4:"flac";}i:10;a:2:{i:0;s:12:"audio/x-flac";i:1;s:10:"audio/flac";}i:11;s:13:"org.xiph.flac";i:12;s:32:"FLAC (Free Lossless Audio Codec)";i:13;s:5:"1.2.1";i:14;N;i:15;s:7:"fmt/279";i:16;N;i:17;s:5:"Audio";i:18;N;i:19;s:648:"FLAC (Free Lossless Audio Codec) is an audio coding format for lossless compression of digital audio, developed by the Xiph.Org Foundation, and is also the name of the free software project producing the FLAC tools, the reference software package that includes a codec implementation. Digital audio compressed by FLAC's algorithm can typically be reduced to between 50 and 70 percent of its original size and decompress to an identical copy of the original audio data. FLAC is an open format with royalty-free licensing and a reference implementation which is free software. FLAC has support for metadata tagging, album cover art, and fast seeking.";i:20;N;i:21;N;i:22;a:3:{i:0;s:123:"https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/PRONOM/Format/proFormatSearch.aspx?status=detailReport&id=1019&strPageToDisplay=summary";i:1;s:34:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/FLAC";i:2;s:33:"https://xiph.org/flac/format.html";}}i:89;a:23:{i:0;i:0;i:1;i:8;i:2;s:1:"s";i:3;s:8:"Rar!␀";i:4;i:0;i:5;i:0;i:6;s:1:"s";i:7;s:0:"";i:8;s:3:"rar";i:9;a:1:{i:0;s:3:"rar";}i:10;a:2:{i:0;s:28:"application/x-rar-compressed";i:1;s:19:"application/vnd.rar";}i:11;s:22:"com.rarlab.rar-archive";i:12;s:11:"RAR Archive";i:13;s:3:"5.0";i:14;N;i:15;s:7:"fmt/613";i:16;N;i:17;s:9:"Aggregate";i:18;N;i:19;s:106:"RAR is a proprietary archive file format that supports data compression, error recovery and file spanning.";i:20;N;i:21;N;i:22;a:2:{i:0;s:123:"https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/PRONOM/Format/proFormatSearch.aspx?status=detailReport&id=1409&strPageToDisplay=summary";i:1;s:47:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/RAR_(file_format)";}}i:90;a:23:{i:0;i:0;i:1;i:7;i:2;s:1:"s";i:3;s:7:"ELF";i:4;i:0;i:5;i:0;i:6;s:1:"s";i:7;s:0:"";i:8;s:0:"";i:9;a:10:{i:0;s:0:"";i:1;s:3:"axf";i:2;s:3:"bin";i:3;s:3:"elf";i:4;s:2:"ko";i:5;s:3:"mod";i:6;s:1:"o";i:7;s:3:"prx";i:8;s:4:"puff";i:9;s:2:"so";}i:10;s:24:"application/octet-stream";i:11;N;i:12;s:30:"Executable and Linkable Format";i:13;s:16:"32bit Big Endian";i:14;N;i:15;s:7:"fmt/689";i:16;N;i:17;N;i:18;N;i:19;s:586:"In computing, the Executable and Linkable Format (ELF, formerly named Extensible Linking Format), is a common standard file format for executable files, object code, shared libraries, and core dumps. First published in the specification for the application binary interface (ABI) of the Unix operating system version named System V Release 4 (SVR4), and later in the Tool Interface Standard, it was quickly accepted among different vendors of Unix systems. In 1999, it was chosen as the standard binary file format for Unix and Unix-like systems on x86 processors by the 86open project.";i:20;N;i:21;N;i:22;a:4:{i:0;s:123:"https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/PRONOM/Format/proFormatSearch.aspx?status=detailReport&id=1488&strPageToDisplay=summary";i:1;s:60:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Executable_and_Linkable_Format";i:2;s:91:"https://refspecs.linuxfoundation.org/LSB_4.1.0/LSB-Core-AMD64/LSB-Core-AMD64/elf-amd64.html";i:3;s:62:"http://www.sco.com/developers/gabi/2000-07-17/ch4.eheader.html";}}i:91;a:23:{i:0;i:0;i:1;i:7;i:2;s:1:"s";i:3;s:7:"ELF";i:4;i:0;i:5;i:0;i:6;s:1:"s";i:7;s:0:"";i:8;s:0:"";i:9;a:10:{i:0;s:0:"";i:1;s:3:"axf";i:2;s:3:"bin";i:3;s:3:"elf";i:4;s:2:"ko";i:5;s:3:"mod";i:6;s:1:"o";i:7;s:3:"prx";i:8;s:4:"puff";i:9;s:2:"so";}i:10;s:24:"application/octet-stream";i:11;N;i:12;s:30:"Executable and Linkable Format";i:13;s:19:"32bit Little Endian";i:14;N;i:15;s:7:"fmt/688";i:16;N;i:17;N;i:18;N;i:19;s:586:"In computing, the Executable and Linkable Format (ELF, formerly named Extensible Linking Format), is a common standard file format for executable files, object code, shared libraries, and core dumps. First published in the specification for the application binary interface (ABI) of the Unix operating system version named System V Release 4 (SVR4), and later in the Tool Interface Standard, it was quickly accepted among different vendors of Unix systems. In 1999, it was chosen as the standard binary file format for Unix and Unix-like systems on x86 processors by the 86open project.";i:20;N;i:21;N;i:22;a:4:{i:0;s:123:"https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/PRONOM/Format/proFormatSearch.aspx?status=detailReport&id=1487&strPageToDisplay=summary";i:1;s:60:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Executable_and_Linkable_Format";i:2;s:91:"https://refspecs.linuxfoundation.org/LSB_4.1.0/LSB-Core-AMD64/LSB-Core-AMD64/elf-amd64.html";i:3;s:62:"http://www.sco.com/developers/gabi/2000-07-17/ch4.eheader.html";}}i:92;a:23:{i:0;i:0;i:1;i:7;i:2;s:1:"s";i:3;s:7:"ELF";i:4;i:0;i:5;i:0;i:6;s:1:"s";i:7;s:0:"";i:8;s:0:"";i:9;a:10:{i:0;s:0:"";i:1;s:3:"axf";i:2;s:3:"bin";i:3;s:3:"elf";i:4;s:2:"ko";i:5;s:3:"mod";i:6;s:1:"o";i:7;s:3:"prx";i:8;s:4:"puff";i:9;s:2:"so";}i:10;s:24:"application/octet-stream";i:11;N;i:12;s:30:"Executable and Linkable Format";i:13;s:16:"64bit Big Endian";i:14;N;i:15;s:7:"fmt/691";i:16;N;i:17;N;i:18;N;i:19;s:586:"In computing, the Executable and Linkable Format (ELF, formerly named Extensible Linking Format), is a common standard file format for executable files, object code, shared libraries, and core dumps. First published in the specification for the application binary interface (ABI) of the Unix operating system version named System V Release 4 (SVR4), and later in the Tool Interface Standard, it was quickly accepted among different vendors of Unix systems. In 1999, it was chosen as the standard binary file format for Unix and Unix-like systems on x86 processors by the 86open project.";i:20;N;i:21;N;i:22;a:4:{i:0;s:123:"https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/PRONOM/Format/proFormatSearch.aspx?status=detailReport&id=1490&strPageToDisplay=summary";i:1;s:60:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Executable_and_Linkable_Format";i:2;s:91:"https://refspecs.linuxfoundation.org/LSB_4.1.0/LSB-Core-AMD64/LSB-Core-AMD64/elf-amd64.html";i:3;s:62:"http://www.sco.com/developers/gabi/2000-07-17/ch4.eheader.html";}}i:93;a:23:{i:0;i:0;i:1;i:7;i:2;s:1:"s";i:3;s:7:"ELF";i:4;i:0;i:5;i:0;i:6;s:1:"s";i:7;s:0:"";i:8;s:0:"";i:9;a:10:{i:0;s:0:"";i:1;s:3:"axf";i:2;s:3:"bin";i:3;s:3:"elf";i:4;s:2:"ko";i:5;s:3:"mod";i:6;s:1:"o";i:7;s:3:"prx";i:8;s:4:"puff";i:9;s:2:"so";}i:10;s:24:"application/octet-stream";i:11;N;i:12;s:30:"Executable and Linkable Format";i:13;s:19:"64bit Little Endian";i:14;N;i:15;s:7:"fmt/690";i:16;N;i:17;N;i:18;N;i:19;s:586:"In computing, the Executable and Linkable Format (ELF, formerly named Extensible Linking Format), is a common standard file format for executable files, object code, shared libraries, and core dumps. First published in the specification for the application binary interface (ABI) of the Unix operating system version named System V Release 4 (SVR4), and later in the Tool Interface Standard, it was quickly accepted among different vendors of Unix systems. In 1999, it was chosen as the standard binary file format for Unix and Unix-like systems on x86 processors by the 86open project.";i:20;N;i:21;N;i:22;a:4:{i:0;s:123:"https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/PRONOM/Format/proFormatSearch.aspx?status=detailReport&id=1489&strPageToDisplay=summary";i:1;s:60:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Executable_and_Linkable_Format";i:2;s:91:"https://refspecs.linuxfoundation.org/LSB_4.1.0/LSB-Core-AMD64/LSB-Core-AMD64/elf-amd64.html";i:3;s:62:"http://www.sco.com/developers/gabi/2000-07-17/ch4.eheader.html";}}i:94;a:23:{i:0;i:0;i:1;i:7;i:2;s:1:"s";i:3;s:7:"!<arch>";i:4;i:0;i:5;i:0;i:6;s:1:"s";i:7;s:0:"";i:8;s:3:"deb";i:9;a:2:{i:0;s:3:"deb";i:1;s:4:"udeb";}i:10;a:3:{i:0;s:37:"application/vnd.debian.binary-package";i:1;s:28:"application/x-debian-package";i:2;s:17:"application/x-deb";}i:11;N;i:12;s:14:"Debian package";i:13;N;i:14;N;i:15;N;i:16;N;i:17;N;i:18;N;i:19;s:125:"deb is the format, as well as extension of the software package format for the Linux distribution Debian and its derivatives.";i:20;N;i:21;N;i:22;a:2:{i:0;s:47:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deb_(file_format)";i:1;s:59:"https://manpages.debian.org/unstable/dpkg-dev/deb.5.en.html";}}i:95;a:23:{i:0;i:0;i:1;i:7;i:2;s:2:"sr";i:3;s:48:"\x50\x34[\x20\x0d\x0a][\x30-\x39\x20\x0a\x0d]{4}";i:4;i:0;i:5;i:0;i:6;s:1:"s";i:7;s:0:"";i:8;s:3:"pbm";i:9;a:2:{i:0;s:3:"pbm";i:1;s:4:"pbmb";}i:10;s:23:"image/x-portable-bitmap";i:11;N;i:12;s:46:"Portable Bitmap Image - Binary without comment";i:13;N;i:14;N;i:15;s:7:"fmt/409";i:16;N;i:17;N;i:18;N;i:19;s:400:"Several graphics formats are used and defined by the Netpbm project. The portable pixmap format (PPM), the portable graymap format (PGM) and the portable bitmap format (PBM) are image file formats designed to be easily exchanged between platforms. They are also sometimes referred to collectively as the portable anymap format (PNM), not to be confused with the related portable arbitrary map format.";i:20;s:6:"Binary";i:21;N;i:22;a:2:{i:0;s:123:"https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/PRONOM/Format/proFormatSearch.aspx?status=detailReport&id=1157&strPageToDisplay=summary";i:1;s:36:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Netpbm";}}i:96;a:23:{i:0;i:0;i:1;i:7;i:2;s:1:"s";i:3;s:7:"Rar!␀";i:4;i:0;i:5;i:0;i:6;s:1:"s";i:7;s:0:"";i:8;s:3:"rar";i:9;a:1:{i:0;s:3:"rar";}i:10;a:2:{i:0;s:28:"application/x-rar-compressed";i:1;s:19:"application/vnd.rar";}i:11;s:22:"com.rarlab.rar-archive";i:12;s:11:"RAR Archive";i:13;s:7:"1.5-4.0";i:14;N;i:15;N;i:16;N;i:17;N;i:18;N;i:19;s:106:"RAR is a proprietary archive file format that supports data compression, error recovery and file spanning.";i:20;N;i:21;N;i:22;a:1:{i:0;s:47:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/RAR_(file_format)";}}i:97;a:23:{i:0;i:0;i:1;i:6;i:2;s:1:"s";i:3;s:6:"7z¼¯'";i:4;i:0;i:5;i:0;i:6;s:1:"s";i:7;s:0:"";i:8;s:2:"7z";i:9;a:1:{i:0;s:2:"7z";}i:10;s:27:"application/x-7z-compressed";i:11;s:23:"org.7-zip.7-zip-archive";i:12;s:11:"7Zip format";i:13;N;i:14;N;i:15;s:7:"fmt/484";i:16;N;i:17;s:9:"Aggregate";i:18;N;i:19;s:299:"7z is a compressed archive file format that supports several different data compression, encryption and pre-processing algorithms. The 7z format initially appeared as implemented by the 7-Zip archiver. The 7-Zip program is publicly available under the terms of the GNU Lesser General Public License.";i:20;N;i:21;N;i:22;a:3:{i:0;s:123:"https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/PRONOM/Format/proFormatSearch.aspx?status=detailReport&id=1271&strPageToDisplay=summary";i:1;s:32:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/7z";i:2;s:29:"https://www.7-zip.org/7z.html";}}i:98;a:23:{i:0;i:0;i:1;i:6;i:2;s:1:"s";i:3;s:6:"GIF87a";i:4;i:0;i:5;i:0;i:6;s:1:"s";i:7;s:0:"";i:8;s:3:"gif";i:9;a:1:{i:0;s:3:"gif";}i:10;s:9:"image/gif";i:11;s:18:"com.compuserve.gif";i:12;s:33:"Graphics Interchange Format (GIF)";i:13;s:3:"87a";i:14;s:11:"GIF (1987a)";i:15;s:5:"fmt/3";i:16;N;i:17;s:14:"Image (Raster)";i:18;s:4:"Full";i:19;s:891:"The Graphics Interchange Format (GIF) is a bitmap image format that was developed by a team at the online services provider CompuServe led by American computer scientist Steve Wilhite on June 15, 1987. It has since come into widespread usage on the World Wide Web due to its wide support and portability between applications and operating systems. The format supports up to 8 bits per pixel for each image, allowing a single image to reference its own palette of up to 256 different colors chosen from the 24-bit RGB color space. It also supports animations and allows a separate palette of up to 256 colors for each frame. These palette limitations make GIF less suitable for reproducing color photographs and other images with color gradients, but well-suited for simpler images such as graphics or logos with solid areas of color. Unlike video, the GIF file format does not support audio.";i:20;s:6:"Binary";i:21;s:21:"Little-endian (Intel)";i:22;a:3:{i:0;s:122:"https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/PRONOM/Format/proFormatSearch.aspx?status=detailReport&id=619&strPageToDisplay=summary";i:1;s:33:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/GIF";i:2;s:46:"https://www.w3.org/Graphics/GIF/spec-gif87.txt";}}i:99;a:23:{i:0;i:0;i:1;i:6;i:2;s:1:"s";i:3;s:6:"GIF89a";i:4;i:0;i:5;i:0;i:6;s:1:"s";i:7;s:0:"";i:8;s:3:"gif";i:9;a:1:{i:0;s:3:"gif";}i:10;s:9:"image/gif";i:11;s:18:"com.compuserve.gif";i:12;s:33:"Graphics Interchange Format (GIF)";i:13;s:3:"89a";i:14;s:11:"GIF (1989a)";i:15;s:5:"fmt/4";i:16;N;i:17;s:14:"Image (Raster)";i:18;s:4:"Full";i:19;s:891:"The Graphics Interchange Format (GIF) is a bitmap image format that was developed by a team at the online services provider CompuServe led by American computer scientist Steve Wilhite on June 15, 1987. It has since come into widespread usage on the World Wide Web due to its wide support and portability between applications and operating systems. The format supports up to 8 bits per pixel for each image, allowing a single image to reference its own palette of up to 256 different colors chosen from the 24-bit RGB color space. It also supports animations and allows a separate palette of up to 256 colors for each frame. These palette limitations make GIF less suitable for reproducing color photographs and other images with color gradients, but well-suited for simpler images such as graphics or logos with solid areas of color. Unlike video, the GIF file format does not support audio.";i:20;s:6:"Binary";i:21;s:21:"Little-endian (Intel)";i:22;a:3:{i:0;s:122:"https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/PRONOM/Format/proFormatSearch.aspx?status=detailReport&id=620&strPageToDisplay=summary";i:1;s:33:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/GIF";i:2;s:47:"https://www.w3.org/Graphics/GIF/spec-gif89a.txt";}}i:100;a:23:{i:0;i:0;i:1;i:6;i:2;s:1:"s";i:3;s:6:"í«îÛ␀";i:4;i:0;i:5;i:0;i:6;s:1:"s";i:7;s:0:"";i:8;s:3:"rpm";i:9;a:1:{i:0;s:3:"rpm";}i:10;N;i:11;N;i:12;s:24:"RPM Package Manager file";i:13;s:1:"3";i:14;N;i:15;s:7:"fmt/795";i:16;N;i:17;N;i:18;N;i:19;s:611:"RPM Package Manager file (RPM) was previously known as a Red Hat Package Manager File. It is a file used by RPM Package Manager which is a package management system. RPM files currently appear in two defined types, the first is binary package files containing the compiled version of certain software. The second is source package files containing the source code used to produce a package. These have an appropriate tag in the file header that distinguishes them from Binary RPMs, causing them to be extracted to /usr/src on installation. Source package files customarily carry the file extension â€œ.src.rpm".";i:20;N;i:21;N;i:22;a:2:{i:0;s:123:"https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/PRONOM/Format/proFormatSearch.aspx?status=detailReport&id=1594&strPageToDisplay=summary";i:1;s:49:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/RPM_Package_Manager";}}i:101;a:23:{i:0;i:0;i:1;i:6;i:2;s:1:"s";i:3;s:6:"{\rtf1";i:4;i:0;i:5;i:0;i:6;s:1:"s";i:7;s:0:"";i:8;s:3:"rtf";i:9;a:1:{i:0;s:3:"rtf";}i:10;a:2:{i:0;s:15:"application/rtf";i:1;s:8:"text/rtf";}i:11;s:10:"public.rtf";i:12;s:16:"Rich Text Format";i:13;N;i:14;N;i:15;N;i:16;s:16:"Rich Text Format";i:17;s:14:"Word Processor";i:18;s:4:"Full";i:19;s:564:"Rich Text Format (RTF) is a format developed by Microsoft to encode formatted text and graphics for use within applications or for data and formatting transfer between applications. It is closely associated with Microsoft Word, and new versions are developed with each release of Word. RTF files are encoded in plain text, typically ASCII, and consists of a series of control words, control symbols and groups. An RTF file comprises a Header section, containing various header tables, followed by a Document section, containing the document content and formatting.";i:20;s:4:"Text";i:21;N;i:22;a:8:{i:0;s:46:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rich_Text_Format";i:1;s:49:"http://latex2rtf.sourceforge.net/RTF-Spec-1.0.txt";i:2;s:49:"http://latex2rtf.sourceforge.net/RTF-Spec-1.2.pdf";i:3;s:50:"http://www.snake.net/software/RTF/RTF-Spec-1.3.rtf";i:4;s:49:"http://latex2rtf.sourceforge.net/RTF-Spec-1.3.txt";i:5;s:41:"http://www.biblioscape.com/rtf15_spec.htm";i:6;s:90:"https://interoperability.blob.core.windows.net/files/Archive_References/%5bMSFT-RTF%5d.pdf";i:7;s:117:"https://web.archive.org/web/20190708132914if_/http://www.kleinlercher.at/tools/Windows_Protocols/Word2007RTFSpec9.pdf";}}i:102;a:23:{i:0;i:0;i:1;i:6;i:2;s:1:"s";i:3;s:6:"xar!␀";i:4;i:0;i:5;i:0;i:6;s:1:"s";i:7;s:0:"";i:8;s:3:"xar";i:9;a:3:{i:0;s:3:"xar";i:1;s:3:"pkg";i:2;s:3:"xip";}i:10;s:17:"application/x-xar";i:11;s:21:"com.apple.xar-archive";i:12;s:25:"eXtensible ARchive format";i:13;N;i:14;N;i:15;s:7:"fmt/600";i:16;N;i:17;s:9:"Aggregate";i:18;N;i:19;s:318:"XAR (short for eXtensible ARchive format) is an open source file archiver and the archiverâ€™s file format. It was created within the OpenDarwin project and is used in macOS X 10.5 and up for software installation routines, as well as browser extensions in Safari 5.0 and up. Xar replaced the use of gzipped pax files.";i:20;N;i:21;N;i:22;a:2:{i:0;s:123:"https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/PRONOM/Format/proFormatSearch.aspx?status=detailReport&id=1392&strPageToDisplay=summary";i:1;s:44:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Xar_(archiver)";}}i:103;a:23:{i:0;i:0;i:1;i:6;i:2;s:1:"s";i:3;s:6:"ý7zXZ␀";i:4;i:0;i:5;i:0;i:6;s:1:"s";i:7;s:0:"";i:8;s:2:"xz";i:9;a:1:{i:0;s:2:"xz";}i:10;s:16:"application/x-xz";i:11;N;i:12;s:14:"XZ File Format";i:13;N;i:14;N;i:15;s:8:"fmt/1098";i:16;N;i:17;s:9:"Aggregate";i:18;N;i:19;s:232:"The .xz file format is a container format for compressed streams. There are no archiving capabilities, that is, the .xz format can hold only a single file just like the .gz and .bz2 file formats used by gzip and bzip2, respectively.";i:20;N;i:21;N;i:22;a:3:{i:0;s:123:"https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/PRONOM/Format/proFormatSearch.aspx?status=detailReport&id=1907&strPageToDisplay=summary";i:1;s:34:"https://tukaani.org/xz/format.html";i:2;s:41:"https://tukaani.org/xz/xz-file-format.txt";}}i:104;a:23:{i:0;i:0;i:1;i:5;i:2;s:2:"sr";i:3;s:56:"(\x46\x4f\x52\x4d|\x4c\x49\x53\x54|\x43\x41\x54\x20)\x00";i:4;i:0;i:5;i:0;i:6;s:1:"s";i:7;s:0:"";i:8;s:3:"iff";i:9;a:1:{i:0;s:3:"iff";}i:10;N;i:11;N;i:12;s:16:"Interchange File";i:13;N;i:14;N;i:15;s:9:"x-fmt/157";i:16;N;i:17;s:9:"Aggregate";i:18;N;i:19;s:399:"This is an outline record only, and requires further details, research or authentication to provide information that will enable users to further understand the format and to assess digital preservation risks associated with it if appropriate. If you are able to help by supplying any additional information concerning this entry, please return to the main PRONOM page and select â€˜Add an Entryâ€™.";i:20;s:4:"Text";i:21;N;i:22;a:2:{i:0;s:122:"https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/PRONOM/Format/proFormatSearch.aspx?status=detailReport&id=221&strPageToDisplay=summary";i:1;s:53:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Interchange_File_Format";}}i:105;a:23:{i:0;i:0;i:1;i:5;i:2;s:1:"s";i:3;s:5:"í«îÛ";i:4;i:0;i:5;i:0;i:6;s:1:"s";i:7;s:0:"";i:8;s:3:"rpm";i:9;a:1:{i:0;s:3:"rpm";}i:10;N;i:11;N;i:12;s:24:"RPM Package Manager file";i:13;s:1:"1";i:14;N;i:15;s:7:"fmt/793";i:16;N;i:17;N;i:18;N;i:19;s:611:"RPM Package Manager file (RPM) was previously known as a Red Hat Package Manager File. It is a file used by RPM Package Manager which is a package management system. RPM files currently appear in two defined types, the first is binary package files containing the compiled version of certain software. The second is source package files containing the source code used to produce a package. These have an appropriate tag in the file header that distinguishes them from Binary RPMs, causing them to be extracted to /usr/src on installation. Source package files customarily carry the file extension â€œ.src.rpm".";i:20;N;i:21;N;i:22;a:2:{i:0;s:123:"https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/PRONOM/Format/proFormatSearch.aspx?status=detailReport&id=1592&strPageToDisplay=summary";i:1;s:49:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/RPM_Package_Manager";}}i:106;a:23:{i:0;i:0;i:1;i:5;i:2;s:1:"s";i:3;s:5:"í«îÛ";i:4;i:0;i:5;i:0;i:6;s:1:"s";i:7;s:0:"";i:8;s:3:"rpm";i:9;a:1:{i:0;s:3:"rpm";}i:10;N;i:11;N;i:12;s:24:"RPM Package Manager file";i:13;s:1:"2";i:14;N;i:15;s:7:"fmt/794";i:16;N;i:17;N;i:18;N;i:19;s:611:"RPM Package Manager file (RPM) was previously known as a Red Hat Package Manager File. It is a file used by RPM Package Manager which is a package management system. RPM files currently appear in two defined types, the first is binary package files containing the compiled version of certain software. The second is source package files containing the source code used to produce a package. These have an appropriate tag in the file header that distinguishes them from Binary RPMs, causing them to be extracted to /usr/src on installation. Source package files customarily carry the file extension â€œ.src.rpm".";i:20;N;i:21;N;i:22;a:2:{i:0;s:123:"https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/PRONOM/Format/proFormatSearch.aspx?status=detailReport&id=1593&strPageToDisplay=summary";i:1;s:49:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/RPM_Package_Manager";}}i:107;a:23:{i:0;i:0;i:1;i:4;i:2;s:1:"s";i:3;s:4:"%PDF";i:4;i:0;i:5;i:1024;i:6;s:2:"sr";i:7;s:39:"\x25\x25\x45\x4f\x46[\x00-\xff]{0,1019}";i:8;s:3:"pdf";i:9;a:1:{i:0;s:3:"pdf";}i:10;s:15:"application/pdf";i:11;s:13:"com.adobe.pdf";i:12;s:38:"Acrobat PDF - Portable Document Format";i:13;N;i:14;N;i:15;N;i:16;N;i:17;s:16:"Page Description";i:18;N;i:19;s:543:"The Portable Document Format (PDF) is a file format developed by Adobe in 1993 to present documents, including text formatting and images, in a manner independent of application software, hardware, and operating systems. Based on the PostScript language, each PDF file encapsulates a complete description of a fixed-layout flat document, including the text, fonts, vector graphics, raster images and other information needed to display it. PDF was standardized as ISO 32000 in 2008, and no longer requires any royalties for its implementation.";i:20;N;i:21;N;i:22;a:10:{i:0;s:33:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/PDF";i:1;s:76:"https://www.adobe.com/content/dam/acom/en/devnet/pdf/pdfs/PDFReference13.pdf";i:2;s:91:"https://www.adobe.com/content/dam/acom/en/devnet/pdf/pdf_reference_archive/PDFReference.pdf";i:3;s:96:"https://www.adobe.com/content/dam/acom/en/devnet/pdf/pdf_reference_archive/PDFReference15_v6.pdf";i:4;s:93:"https://www.adobe.com/content/dam/acom/en/devnet/pdf/pdf_reference_archive/PDFReference16.pdf";i:5;s:83:"https://www.adobe.com/content/dam/acom/en/devnet/acrobat/pdfs/pdf_reference_1-7.pdf";i:6;s:79:"https://www.adobe.com/content/dam/acom/en/devnet/acrobat/pdfs/PDF32000_2008.pdf";i:7;s:128:"https://web.archive.org/web/20130902000323if_/http://cdn.parleys.com/p/5148922a0364bc17fc56c6e5/iSUM2012_00_LRO_presentation.pdf";i:8;s:111:"https://web.archive.org/web/20180730100811if_/https://www.immagic.com/eLibrary/ARCHIVES/TECH/ADOBE/A070914X.pdf";i:9;s:71:"http://www.cs.nott.ac.uk/~psadb1/Publications/Download/2002/Hardy02.pdf";}}i:108;a:23:{i:0;i:0;i:1;i:4;i:2;s:1:"s";i:3;s:4:"FWS";i:4;i:0;i:5;i:2;i:6;s:1:"s";i:7;s:2:"␀␀";i:8;s:3:"swf";i:9;a:1:{i:0;s:3:"swf";}i:10;s:29:"application/x-shockwave-flash";i:11;N;i:12;s:22:"Small Web Format (SWF)";i:13;s:1:"1";i:14;N;i:15;s:7:"fmt/104";i:16;N;i:17;N;i:18;N;i:19;s:373:"SWF is an Adobe Flash file format used for multimedia, vector graphics and ActionScript. Originating with FutureWave Software, then transferred to Macromedia, and then coming under the control of Adobe, SWF files can contain animations or applets of varying degrees of interactivity and function. They may also occur in programs, commonly browser games, using ActionScript.";i:20;N;i:21;N;i:22;a:3:{i:0;s:122:"https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/PRONOM/Format/proFormatSearch.aspx?status=detailReport&id=646&strPageToDisplay=summary";i:1;s:33:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SWF";i:2;s:41:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Adobe_Flash";}}i:109;a:23:{i:0;i:0;i:1;i:4;i:2;s:1:"s";i:3;s:4:"FWS";i:4;i:0;i:5;i:2;i:6;s:1:"s";i:7;s:2:"␀␀";i:8;s:3:"swf";i:9;a:1:{i:0;s:3:"swf";}i:10;s:29:"application/x-shockwave-flash";i:11;N;i:12;s:22:"Small Web Format (SWF)";i:13;s:1:"2";i:14;N;i:15;s:7:"fmt/105";i:16;N;i:17;N;i:18;N;i:19;s:373:"SWF is an Adobe Flash file format used for multimedia, vector graphics and ActionScript. Originating with FutureWave Software, then transferred to Macromedia, and then coming under the control of Adobe, SWF files can contain animations or applets of varying degrees of interactivity and function. They may also occur in programs, commonly browser games, using ActionScript.";i:20;N;i:21;N;i:22;a:3:{i:0;s:122:"https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/PRONOM/Format/proFormatSearch.aspx?status=detailReport&id=647&strPageToDisplay=summary";i:1;s:33:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SWF";i:2;s:41:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Adobe_Flash";}}i:110;a:23:{i:0;i:0;i:1;i:4;i:2;s:1:"s";i:3;s:4:"FWS";i:4;i:0;i:5;i:2;i:6;s:1:"s";i:7;s:2:"␀␀";i:8;s:3:"swf";i:9;a:1:{i:0;