 of varying degrees of interactivity and function. They may also occur in programs, commonly browser games, using ActionScript.";i:20;N;i:21;N;i:22;a:3:{i:0;s:123:"https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/PRONOM/Format/proFormatSearch.aspx?status=detailReport&id=1574&strPageToDisplay=summary";i:1;s:33:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SWF";i:2;s:41:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Adobe_Flash";}}i:137;a:23:{i:0;i:0;i:1;i:4;i:2;s:1:"s";i:3;s:4:"FWS";i:4;i:0;i:5;i:2;i:6;s:1:"s";i:7;s:2:"␀␀";i:8;s:3:"swf";i:9;a:1:{i:0;s:3:"swf";}i:10;s:29:"application/x-shockwave-flash";i:11;N;i:12;s:22:"Small Web Format (SWF)";i:13;s:2:"30";i:14;N;i:15;s:7:"fmt/776";i:16;N;i:17;N;i:18;N;i:19;s:373:"SWF is an Adobe Flash file format used for multimedia, vector graphics and ActionScript. Originating with FutureWave Software, then transferred to Macromedia, and then coming under the control of Adobe, SWF files can contain animations or applets of varying degrees of interactivity and function. They may also occur in programs, commonly browser games, using ActionScript.";i:20;N;i:21;N;i:22;a:3:{i:0;s:123:"https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/PRONOM/Format/proFormatSearch.aspx?status=detailReport&id=1575&strPageToDisplay=summary";i:1;s:33:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SWF";i:2;s:41:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Adobe_Flash";}}i:138;a:23:{i:0;i:0;i:1;i:4;i:2;s:1:"s";i:3;s:4:"þíúÎ";i:4;i:0;i:5;i:0;i:6;s:1:"s";i:7;s:0:"";i:8;s:0:"";i:9;a:4:{i:0;s:0:"";i:1;s:6:"bundle";i:2;s:5:"dylib";i:3;s:1:"o";}i:10;s:24:"application/octet-stream";i:11;s:23:"com.apple.mach-o-binary";i:12;s:6:"Mach-O";i:13;s:7:"32bit 1";i:14;N;i:15;s:7:"fmt/692";i:16;N;i:17;N;i:18;N;i:19;s:449:"Mach-O, short for Mach object file format, is a file format for executables, object code, shared libraries, dynamically-loaded code, and core dumps. A replacement for the a.out format, Mach-O offers more extensibility and faster access to information in the symbol table. Mach-O is used by most systems based on the Mach kernel. NeXTSTEP, macOS, and iOS are examples of systems that use this format for native executables, libraries and object code.";i:20;N;i:21;N;i:22;a:2:{i:0;s:123:"https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/PRONOM/Format/proFormatSearch.aspx?status=detailReport&id=1491&strPageToDisplay=summary";i:1;s:36:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mach-O";}}i:139;a:23:{i:0;i:0;i:1;i:4;i:2;s:1:"s";i:3;s:4:"Îúíþ";i:4;i:0;i:5;i:0;i:6;s:1:"s";i:7;s:0:"";i:8;s:0:"";i:9;a:4:{i:0;s:0:"";i:1;s:6:"bundle";i:2;s:5:"dylib";i:3;s:1:"o";}i:10;s:24:"application/octet-stream";i:11;s:23:"com.apple.mach-o-binary";i:12;s:6:"Mach-O";i:13;s:7:"32bit 2";i:14;N;i:15;s:7:"fmt/692";i:16;N;i:17;N;i:18;N;i:19;s:449:"Mach-O, short for Mach object file format, is a file format for executables, object code, shared libraries, dynamically-loaded code, and core dumps. A replacement for the a.out format, Mach-O offers more extensibility and faster access to information in the symbol table. Mach-O is used by most systems based on the Mach kernel. NeXTSTEP, macOS, and iOS are examples of systems that use this format for native executables, libraries and object code.";i:20;N;i:21;N;i:22;a:2:{i:0;s:123:"https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/PRONOM/Format/proFormatSearch.aspx?status=detailReport&id=1491&strPageToDisplay=summary";i:1;s:36:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mach-O";}}i:140;a:23:{i:0;i:0;i:1;i:4;i:2;s:1:"s";i:3;s:4:"þíúÏ";i:4;i:0;i:5;i:0;i:6;s:1:"s";i:7;s:0:"";i:8;s:0:"";i:9;a:4:{i:0;s:0:"";i:1;s:6:"bundle";i:2;s:5:"dylib";i:3;s:1:"o";}i:10;s:24:"application/octet-stream";i:11;s:23:"com.apple.mach-o-binary";i:12;s:6:"Mach-O";i:13;s:7:"64bit 1";i:14;N;i:15;s:7:"fmt/693";i:16;N;i:17;N;i:18;N;i:19;s:449:"Mach-O, short for Mach object file format, is a file format for executables, object code, shared libraries, dynamically-loaded code, and core dumps. A replacement for the a.out format, Mach-O offers more extensibility and faster access to information in the symbol table. Mach-O is used by most systems based on the Mach kernel. NeXTSTEP, macOS, and iOS are examples of systems that use this format for native executables, libraries and object code.";i:20;N;i:21;N;i:22;a:2:{i:0;s:123:"https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/PRONOM/Format/proFormatSearch.aspx?status=detailReport&id=1492&strPageToDisplay=summary";i:1;s:36:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mach-O";}}i:141;a:23:{i:0;i:0;i:1;i:4;i:2;s:1:"s";i:3;s:4:"Ïúíþ";i:4;i:0;i:5;i:0;i:6;s:1:"s";i:7;s:0:"";i:8;s:0:"";i:9;a:4:{i:0;s:0:"";i:1;s:6:"bundle";i:2;s:5:"dylib";i:3;s:1:"o";}i:10;s:24:"application/octet-stream";i:11;s:23:"com.apple.mach-o-binary";i:12;s:6:"Mach-O";i:13;s:7:"64bit 2";i:14;N;i:15;s:7:"fmt/693";i:16;N;i:17;N;i:18;N;i:19;s:449:"Mach-O, short for Mach object file format, is a file format for executables, object code, shared libraries, dynamically-loaded code, and core dumps. A replacement for the a.out format, Mach-O offers more extensibility and faster access to information in the symbol table. Mach-O is used by most systems based on the Mach kernel. NeXTSTEP, macOS, and iOS are examples of systems that use this format for native executables, libraries and object code.";i:20;N;i:21;N;i:22;a:2:{i:0;s:123:"https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/PRONOM/Format/proFormatSearch.aspx?status=detailReport&id=1492&strPageToDisplay=summary";i:1;s:36:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mach-O";}}i:142;a:23:{i:0;i:0;i:1;i:4;i:2;s:1:"s";i:3;s:4:"ITSF";i:4;i:0;i:5;i:0;i:6;s:1:"s";i:7;s:0:"";i:8;s:3:"chm";i:9;a:3:{i:0;s:3:"chi";i:1;s:3:"chm";i:2;s:3:"chw";}i:10;s:27:"application/vnd.ms-htmlhelp";i:11;N;i:12;s:28:"Microsoft Compiled HTML Help";i:13;N;i:14;N;i:15;s:7:"fmt/634";i:16;N;i:17;s:17:"Text (Structured)";i:18;N;i:19;s:308:"Microsoft Compiled HTML Help is a Microsoft proprietary online help format, consisting of a collection of HTML pages, an index and other navigation tools. The files are compressed and deployed in a binary format with the extension .CHM, for Compiled HTML. The format is often used for software documentation.";i:20;N;i:21;N;i:22;a:4:{i:0;s:123:"https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/PRONOM/Format/proFormatSearch.aspx?status=detailReport&id=1433&strPageToDisplay=summary";i:1;s:58:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Microsoft_Compiled_HTML_Help";i:2;s:42:"http://www.russotto.net/chm/chmformat.html";i:3;s:47:"http://www.nongnu.org/chmspec/latest/index.html";}}i:143;a:23:{i:0;i:0;i:1;i:4;i:2;s:1:"s";i:3;s:4:"␀␀␀";i:4;i:0;i:5;i:0;i:6;s:1:"s";i:7;s:0:"";i:8;s:3:"cur";i:9;a:1:{i:0;s:3:"cur";}i:10;s:18:"image/x-win-bitmap";i:11;s:17:"com.microsoft.cur";i:12;s:24:"Microsoft Windows Cursor";i:13;N;i:14;N;i:15;s:7:"fmt/385";i:16;N;i:17;s:14:"Image (Raster)";i:18;N;i:19;s:407:"The CUR file format is an almost identical image file format for non-animated cursors in Microsoft Windows. The only differences between these two file formats are the bytes used to identify them and the addition of a hotspot in the CUR format header; the hotspot is defined as the pixel offset (in x,y coordinates) from the top-left corner of the cursor image where the user is actually pointing the mouse.";i:20;s:4:"Text";i:21;N;i:22;a:2:{i:0;s:123:"https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/PRONOM/Format/proFormatSearch.aspx?status=detailReport&id=1133&strPageToDisplay=summary";i:1;s:47:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ICO_(file_format)";}}i:144;a:23:{i:0;i:0;i:1;i:4;i:2;s:1:"s";i:3;s:4:"FLV";i:4;i:0;i:5;i:0;i:6;s:1:"s";i:7;s:0:"";i:8;s:3:"flv";i:9;a:1:{i:0;s:3:"flv";}i:10;s:11:"video/x-flv";i:11;N;i:12;s:28:"Macromedia FLV (Flash Video)";i:13;s:1:"1";i:14;N;i:15;s:9:"x-fmt/382";i:16;N;i:17;s:5:"Video";i:18;N;i:19;s:800:"Flash Video is a container file format used to deliver digital video content (e.g., TV shows, movies, etc.) over the Internet using Adobe Flash Player version 6 and newer. Flash Video content may also be embedded within SWF files. There are two different Flash Video file formats: FLV and F4V. The audio and video data within FLV files are encoded in the same way as SWF files. The F4V file format is based on the ISO base media file format, starting with Flash Player 9 update 3. Both formats are supported in Adobe Flash Player and developed by Adobe Systems. FLV was originally developed by Macromedia. In the early 2000s, Flash Video was the de facto standard for web-based streaming video (over RTMP). Users include Hulu, VEVO, Yahoo! Video, metacafe, Reuters.com, and many other news providers.";i:20;s:6:"Binary";i:21;s:21:"Big-endian (Motorola)";i:22;a:4:{i:0;s:122:"https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/PRONOM/Format/proFormatSearch.aspx?status=detailReport&id=653&strPageToDisplay=summary";i:1;s:41:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flash_Video";i:2;s:67:"http://download.macromedia.com/f4v/video_file_format_spec_v10_1.pdf";i:3;s:68:"https://www.loc.gov/preservation/digital/formats/fdd/fdd000131.shtml";}}i:145;a:23:{i:0;i:0;i:1;i:4;i:2;s:1:"s";i:3;s:4:"␀␀␀";i:4;i:0;i:5;i:0;i:6;s:1:"s";i:7;s:0:"";i:8;s:3:"ico";i:9;a:1:{i:0;s:3:"ico";}i:10;s:12:"image/x-icon";i:11;s:17:"com.microsoft.ico";i:12;s:16:"Icon file format";i:13;N;i:14;N;i:15;s:9:"x-fmt/418";i:16;N;i:17;s:14:"Image (Raster)";i:18;N;i:19;s:363:"The ICO file format is an image file format for computer icons in Microsoft Windows. ICO files contain one or more small images at multiple sizes and color depths, such that they may be scaled appropriately. In Windows, all executables that display an icon to the user, on the desktop, in the Start Menu, or in Windows Explorer, must carry the icon in ICO format.";i:20;s:4:"Text";i:21;N;i:22;a:2:{i:0;s:122:"https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/PRONOM/Format/proFormatSearch.aspx?status=detailReport&id=805&strPageToDisplay=summary";i:1;s:47:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ICO_(file_format)";}}i:146;a:23:{i:0;i:0;i:1;i:4;i:2;s:1:"s";i:3;s:4:"ÿOÿQ";i:4;i:0;i:5;i:0;i:6;s:1:"s";i:7;s:0:"";i:8;s:3:"jpc";i:9;a:1:{i:0;s:3:"jpc";}i:10;s:9:"image/jpc";i:11;N;i:12;s:26:"JPC (JPEG 2000 codestream)";i:13;N;i:14;N;i:15;N;i:16;s:9:"JPEG 2000";i:17;s:14:"Image (Raster)";i:18;N;i:19;s:227:"JPEG 2000 codestream (also known as J2K, J2C, or JPEG 2000 Part 1, Core Coding System) is the wavelet-based compressed image format defined in Part 1 of the JPEG 2000 standard. Both lossy and lossless compression are supported.";i:20;N;i:21;N;i:22;a:4:{i:0;s:60:"http://fileformats.archiveteam.org/wiki/JPEG_2000_codestream";i:1;s:39:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/JPEG_2000";i:2;s:49:"http://fileformats.archiveteam.org/wiki/JPEG_2000";i:3;s:26:"https://jpeg.org/jpeg2000/";}}i:147;a:23:{i:0;i:0;i:1;i:4;i:2;s:2:"sr";i:3;s:33:"\x50\x31(\x20|\x09|\x0d\x0a|\x0a)";i:4;i:0;i:5;i:0;i:6;s:1:"s";i:7;s:0:"";i:8;s:3:"pbm";i:9;a:2:{i:0;s:3:"pbm";i:1;s:4:"pbma";}i:10;s:23:"image/x-portable-bitmap";i:11;N;i:12;s:29:"Portable Bitmap Image - ASCII";i:13;N;i:14;N;i:15;s:9:"x-fmt/164";i:16;N;i:17;s:14:"Image (Raster)";i:18;N;i:19;s:400:"Several graphics formats are used and defined by the Netpbm project. The portable pixmap format (PPM), the portable graymap format (PGM) and the portable bitmap format (PBM) are image file formats designed to be easily exchanged between platforms. They are also sometimes referred to collectively as the portable anymap format (PNM), not to be confused with the related portable arbitrary map format.";i:20;s:4:"Text";i:21;N;i:22;a:2:{i:0;s:122:"https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/PRONOM/Format/proFormatSearch.aspx?status=detailReport&id=236&strPageToDisplay=summary";i:1;s:36:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Netpbm";}}i:148;a:23:{i:0;i:0;i:1;i:4;i:2;s:2:"sr";i:3;s:33:"\x50\x32(\x20|\x09|\x0d\x0a|\x0a)";i:4;i:0;i:5;i:0;i:6;s:1:"s";i:7;s:0:"";i:8;s:3:"pgm";i:9;a:2:{i:0;s:3:"pgm";i:1;s:4:"pgma";}i:10;s:24:"image/x-portable-graymap";i:11;N;i:12;s:25:"Portable Grey Map - ASCII";i:13;N;i:14;N;i:15;s:7:"fmt/407";i:16;N;i:17;N;i:18;N;i:19;s:400:"Several graphics formats are used and defined by the Netpbm project. The portable pixmap format (PPM), the portable graymap format (PGM) and the portable bitmap format (PBM) are image file formats designed to be easily exchanged between platforms. They are also sometimes referred to collectively as the portable anymap format (PNM), not to be confused with the related portable arbitrary map format.";i:20;s:4:"Text";i:21;N;i:22;a:2:{i:0;s:123:"https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/PRONOM/Format/proFormatSearch.aspx?status=detailReport&id=1155&strPageToDisplay=summary";i:1;s:36:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Netpbm";}}i:149;a:23:{i:0;i:0;i:1;i:4;i:2;s:2:"sr";i:3;s:33:"\x50\x33(\x20|\x09|\x0d\x0a|\x0a)";i:4;i:0;i:5;i:0;i:6;s:1:"s";i:7;s:0:"";i:8;s:3:"ppm";i:9;a:2:{i:0;s:3:"ppm";i:1;s:4:"ppma";}i:10;s:23:"image/x-portable-pixmap";i:11;N;i:12;s:26:"Portable Pixel Map - ASCII";i:13;N;i:14;N;i:15;s:9:"x-fmt/178";i:16;N;i:17;s:14:"Image (Raster)";i:18;N;i:19;s:400:"Several graphics formats are used and defined by the Netpbm project. The portable pixmap format (PPM), the portable graymap format (PGM) and the portable bitmap format (PBM) are image file formats designed to be easily exchanged between platforms. They are also sometimes referred to collectively as the portable anymap format (PNM), not to be confused with the related portable arbitrary map format.";i:20;s:4:"Text";i:21;N;i:22;a:2:{i:0;s:122:"https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/PRONOM/Format/proFormatSearch.aspx?status=detailReport&id=251&strPageToDisplay=summary";i:1;s:36:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Netpbm";}}i:150;a:23:{i:0;i:0;i:1;i:4;i:2;s:1:"s";i:3;s:4:"CWS";i:4;i:0;i:5;i:0;i:6;s:1:"s";i:7;s:0:"";i:8;s:3:"swf";i:9;a:1:{i:0;s:3:"swf";}i:10;s:29:"application/x-shockwave-flash";i:11;N;i:12;s:22:"Small Web Format (SWF)";i:13;s:19:"6 (zlib compressed)";i:14;N;i:15;s:7:"fmt/109";i:16;N;i:17;N;i:18;N;i:19;s:373:"SWF is an Adobe Flash file format used for multimedia, vector graphics and ActionScript. Originating with FutureWave Software, then transferred to Macromedia, and then coming under the control of Adobe, SWF files can contain animations or applets of varying degrees of interactivity and function. They may also occur in programs, commonly browser games, using ActionScript.";i:2