ionScript. Originating with FutureWave Software, then transferred to Macromedia, and then coming under the control of Adobe, SWF files can contain animations or applets of varying degrees of interactivity and function. They may also occur in programs, commonly browser games, using ActionScript.";i:20;N;i:21;N;i:22;a:3:{i:0;s:123:"https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/PRONOM/Format/proFormatSearch.aspx?status=detailReport&id=1574&strPageToDisplay=summary";i:1;s:33:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SWF";i:2;s:41:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Adobe_Flash";}}i:191;a:23:{i:0;i:0;i:1;i:4;i:2;s:1:"s";i:3;s:4:"CWS";i:4;i:0;i:5;i:0;i:6;s:1:"s";i:7;s:0:"";i:8;s:3:"swf";i:9;a:1:{i:0;s:3:"swf";}i:10;s:29:"application/x-shockwave-flash";i:11;N;i:12;s:22:"Small Web Format (SWF)";i:13;s:20:"30 (zlib compressed)";i:14;N;i:15;s:7:"fmt/776";i:16;N;i:17;N;i:18;N;i:19;s:373:"SWF is an Adobe Flash file format used for multimedia, vector graphics and ActionScript. Originating with FutureWave Software, then transferred to Macromedia, and then coming under the control of Adobe, SWF files can contain animations or applets of varying degrees of interactivity and function. They may also occur in programs, commonly browser games, using ActionScript.";i:20;N;i:21;N;i:22;a:3:{i:0;s:123:"https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/PRONOM/Format/proFormatSearch.aspx?status=detailReport&id=1575&strPageToDisplay=summary";i:1;s:33:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SWF";i:2;s:41:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Adobe_Flash";}}i:192;a:23:{i:0;i:0;i:1;i:4;i:2;s:1:"s";i:3;s:4:"ZWS";i:4;i:0;i:5;i:0;i:6;s:1:"s";i:7;s:0:"";i:8;s:3:"swf";i:9;a:1:{i:0;s:3:"swf";}i:10;s:29:"application/x-shockwave-flash";i:11;N;i:12;s:22:"Small Web Format (SWF)";i:13;s:20:"30 (LZMA compressed)";i:14;N;i:15;s:7:"fmt/776";i:16;N;i:17;N;i:18;N;i:19;s:373:"SWF is an Adobe Flash file format used for multimedia, vector graphics and ActionScript. Originating with FutureWave Software, then transferred to Macromedia, and then coming under the control of Adobe, SWF files can contain animations or applets of varying degrees of interactivity and function. They may also occur in programs, commonly browser games, using ActionScript.";i:20;N;i:21;N;i:22;a:3:{i:0;s:123:"https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/PRONOM/Format/proFormatSearch.aspx?status=detailReport&id=1575&strPageToDisplay=summary";i:1;s:33:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SWF";i:2;s:41:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Adobe_Flash";}}i:193;a:23:{i:0;i:0;i:1;i:4;i:2;s:1:"s";i:3;s:4:"II*␀";i:4;i:0;i:5;i:0;i:6;s:1:"s";i:7;s:0:"";i:8;s:3:"tif";i:9;a:2:{i:0;s:3:"tif";i:1;s:4:"tiff";}i:10;s:10:"image/tiff";i:11;s:11:"public.tiff";i:12;s:24:"Tagged Image File Format";i:13;N;i:14;s:4:"TIFF";i:15;s:7:"fmt/353";i:16;N;i:17;s:14:"Image (Raster)";i:18;s:4:"Full";i:19;s:813:"Tagged Image File Format, abbreviated TIFF or TIF, is a computer file format for storing raster graphics images, popular among graphic artists, the publishing industry, and photographers. TIFF is widely supported by scanning, faxing, word processing, optical character recognition, image manipulation, desktop publishing, and page-layout applications. The format was created by Aldus Corporation for use in desktop publishing. It published the latest version 6.0 in 1992, subsequently updated with an Adobe Systems copyright after the latter acquired Aldus in 1994. Several Aldus or Adobe technical notes have been published with minor extensions to the format, and several specifications have been based on TIFF 6.0, including TIFF/EP (ISO 12234-2), TIFF/IT (ISO 12639), TIFF-F (RFC 2306) and TIFF-FX (RFC 3949).";i:20;N;i:21;s:21:"Little-endian (Intel)";i:22;a:12:{i:0;s:123:"https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/PRONOM/Format/proFormatSearch.aspx?status=detailReport&id=1099&strPageToDisplay=summary";i:1;s:34:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/TIFF";i:2;s:35:"https://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc3302";i:3;s:35:"https://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc3949";i:4;s:35:"https://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc3950";i:5;s:35:"https://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc2306";i:6;s:35:"https://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc1314";i:7;s:64:"https://cool.culturalheritage.org/bytopic/imaging/std/tiff4.html";i:8;s:64:"https://cool.culturalheritage.org/bytopic/imaging/std/tiff5.html";i:9;s:69:"https://www.adobe.io/content/dam/udp/en/open/standards/tiff/TIFF6.pdf";i:10;s:68:"https://www.loc.gov/preservation/digital/formats/fdd/fdd000022.shtml";i:11;s:35:"http://www.libtiff.org/support.html";}}i:194;a:23:{i:0;i:0;i:1;i:4;i:2;s:1:"s";i:3;s:4:"MM␀*";i:4;i:0;i:5;i:0;i:6;s:1:"s";i:7;s:0:"";i:8;s:3:"tif";i:9;a:2:{i:0;s:3:"tif";i:1;s:4:"tiff";}i:10;s:10:"image/tiff";i:11;s:11:"public.tiff";i:12;s:24:"Tagged Image File Format";i:13;N;i:14;s:4:"TIFF";i:15;s:7:"fmt/353";i:16;N;i:17;s:14:"Image (Raster)";i:18;s:4:"Full";i:19;s:813:"Tagged Image File Format, abbreviated TIFF or TIF, is a computer file format for storing raster graphics images, popular among graphic artists, the publishing industry, and photographers. TIFF is widely supported by scanning, faxing, word processing, optical character recognition, image manipulation, desktop publishing, and page-layout applications. The format was created by Aldus Corporation for use in desktop publishing. It published the latest version 6.0 in 1992, subsequently updated with an Adobe Systems copyright after the latter acquired Aldus in 1994. Several Aldus or Adobe technical notes have been published with minor extensions to the format, and several specifications have been based on TIFF 6.0, including TIFF/EP (ISO 12234-2), TIFF/IT (ISO 12639), TIFF-F (RFC 2306) and TIFF-FX (RFC 3949).";i:20;N;i:21;s:21:"Big-endian (Motorola)";i:22;a:12:{i:0;s:123:"https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/PRONOM/Format/proFormatSearch.aspx?status=detailReport&id=1099&strPageToDisplay=summary";i:1;s:34:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/TIFF";i:2;s:35:"https://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc3302";i:3;s:35:"https://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc3949";i:4;s:35:"https://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc3950";i:5;s:35:"https://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc2306";i:6;s:35:"https://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc1314";i:7;s:64:"https://cool.culturalheritage.org/bytopic/imaging/std/tiff4.html";i:8;s:64:"https://cool.culturalheritage.org/bytopic/imaging/std/tiff5.html";i:9;s:69:"https://www.adobe.io/content/dam/udp/en/open/standards/tiff/TIFF6.pdf";i:10;s:68:"https://www.loc.gov/preservation/digital/formats/fdd/fdd000022.shtml";i:11;s:35:"http://www.libtiff.org/support.html";}}i:195;a:23:{i:0;i:0;i:1;i:4;i:2;s:1:"s";i:3;s:4:"wOFF";i:4;i:0;i:5;i:0;i:6;s:1:"s";i:7;s:0:"";i:8;s:4:"woff";i:9;a:1:{i:0;s:4:"woff";}i:10;s:9:"font/woff";i:11;N;i:12;s:20:"Web Open Font Format";i:13;s:3:"1.0";i:14;N;i:15;s:7:"fmt/616";i:16;N;i:17;s:4:"Font";i:18;N;i:19;s:477:"The Web Open Font Format (WOFF) is a font format for use in web pages. WOFF files are OpenType or TrueType fonts, with format-specific compression applied and additional XML metadata added. The two primary goals are to first distinguish font files intended for use as web fonts from fonts files intended for use in desktop applications via local installation, and second to reduce web font latency when fonts are transferred from a server to a client over a network connection.";i:20;N;i:21;N;i:22;a:4:{i:0;s:123:"https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/PRONOM/Format/proFormatSearch.aspx?status=detailReport&id=1412&strPageToDisplay=summary";i:1;s:50:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Web_Open_Font_Format";i:2;s:35:"https://www.w3.org/Submission/WOFF/";i:3;s:27:"https://www.w3.org/TR/WOFF/";}}i:196;a:23:{i:0;i:0;i:1;i:4;i:2;s:1:"s";i:3;s:4:"wOF2";i:4;i:0;i:5;i:0;i:6;s:1:"s";i:7;s:0:"";i:8;s:5:"woff2";i:9;a:1:{i:0;s:5:"woff2";}i:10;s:10:"font/woff2";i:11;N;i:12;s:20:"Web Open Font Format";i:13;s:3:"2.0";i:14;N;i:15;s:8:"fmt/1172";i:16;N;i:17;s:4:"Font";i:18;N;i:19;s:477:"The Web Open Font Format (WOFF) is a font format for use in web pages. WOFF files are OpenType or TrueType fonts, with format-specific compression applied and additional XML metadata added. The two primary goals are to first distinguish font files intended for use as web fonts from fonts files intended for use in desktop applications via local installation, and second to reduce web font latency when fonts are transferred from a server to a client over a network connection.";i:20;N;i:21;N;i:22;a:3:{i:0;s:123:"https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/PRONOM/Format/proFormatSearch.aspx?status=detailReport&id=1982&strPageToDisplay=summary";i:1;s:50:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Web_Open_Font_Format";i:2;s:28:"https://www.w3.org/TR/WOFF2/";}}i:197;a:23:{i:0;i:0;i:1;i:4;i:2;s:2:"sr";i:3;s:38:"\x50\x4b(?:\x03\x04|\x05\x06|\x07\x08)";i:4;i:0;i:5;i:0;i:6;s:1:"s";i:7;s:0:"";i:8;s:3:"zip";i:9;a:1:{i:0;s:3:"zip";}i:10;s:15:"application/zip";i:11;s:22:"com.pkware.zip-archive";i:12;s:10:"ZIP Format";i:13;N;i:14;N;i:15;s:9:"x-fmt/263";i:16;N;i:17;s:9:"Aggregate";i:18;N;i:19;s:862:"ZIP is an archive file format that supports lossless data compression. A ZIP file may contain one or more files or directories that may have been compressed. The ZIP file format permits a number of compression algorithms, though DEFLATE is the most common. This format was originally created in 1989 and was first implemented in PKWARE, Inc.'s PKZIP utility, as a replacement for the previous ARC compression format by Thom Henderson. The ZIP format was then quickly supported by many software utilities other than PKZIP. Microsoft has included built-in ZIP support (under the name "compressed folders") in versions of Microsoft Windows since 1998. Apple has included built-in ZIP support in Mac OS X 10.3 (via BOMArchiveHelper, now Archive Utility) and later. Most free operating systems have built in support for ZIP in similar manners to Windows and Mac OS X.";i:20;s:4:"Text";i:21;N;i:22;a:14:{i:0;s:122:"https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/PRONOM/Format/proFormatSearch.aspx?status=detailReport&id=382&strPageToDisplay=summary";i:1;s:47:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zip_(file_format)";i:2;s:61:"https://pkware.cachefly.net/webdocs/APPNOTE/APPNOTE-5.2.0.txt";i:3;s:61:"https://pkware.cachefly.net/webdocs/APPNOTE/APPNOTE-6.2.0.txt";i:4;s:61:"https://pkware.cachefly.net/webdocs/APPNOTE/APPNOTE-6.3.0.TXT";i:5;s:61:"https://pkware.cachefly.net/webdocs/APPNOTE/APPNOTE-6.3.1.TXT";i:6;s:61:"https://pkware.cachefly.net/webdocs/APPNOTE/APPNOTE-6.3.2.TXT";i:7;s:61:"https://pkware.cachefly.net/webdocs/APPNOTE/APPNOTE-6.3.3.TXT";i:8;s:61:"https://pkware.cachefly.net/webdocs/APPNOTE/APPNOTE-6.3.4.TXT";i:9;s:61:"https://pkware.cachefly.net/webdocs/APPNOTE/APPNOTE-6.3.5.TXT";i:10;s:61:"https://pkware.cachefly.net/webdocs/APPNOTE/APPNOTE-6.3.6.TXT";i:11;s:61:"https://pkware.cachefly.net/webdocs/APPNOTE/APPNOTE-6.3.7.TXT";i:12;s:61:"https://pkware.cachefly.net/webdocs/APPNOTE/APPNOTE-6.3.8.TXT";i:13;s:61:"https://pkware.cachefly.net/webdocs/APPNOTE/APPNOTE-6.3.9.TXT";}}i:198;a:23:{i:0;i:0;i:1;i:4;i:2;s:1:"s";i:3;s:4:"(µ/ý";i:4;i:0;i:5;i:0;i:6;s:1:"s";i:7;s:0:"";i:8;s:3:"zst";i:9;a:1:{i:0;s:3:"zst";}i:10;s:16:"application/zstd";i:11;N;i:12;s:21:"Zstandard Compression";i:13;N;i:14;N;i:15;N;i:16;N;i:17;s:9:"Aggregate";i:18;N;i:19;s:430:"Zstandard is a fast compression algorithm, providing high compression ratios. It also offers a special mode for small data, called dictionary compression. The reference library offers a very wide range of speed / compression trade-off, and is backed by an extremely fast decoder (see benchmarks below). Zstandard library is provided as open source software using a BSD license. Its format is stable and published as IETF RFC 8878.";i:20;N;i:21;N;i:22;a:5:{i:0;s:38:"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8878";i:1;s:34:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zstd";i:2;s:32:"https://facebook.github.io/zstd/";i:3;s:28:"https://youtu.be/k5XsiuxHv_A";i:4;s:32:"https://github.com/facebook/zstd";}}i:199;a:23:{i:0;i:0;i:1;i:3;i:2;s:2:"sr";i:3;s:12:"\xff\xd8\xff";i:4;i:0;i:5;i:2;i:6;s:1:"s";i:7;s:2:"ÿÙ";i:8;s:3:"jpg";i:9;a:3:{i:0;s:3:"jpe";i:1;s:4:"jpeg";i:2;s:3:"jpg";}i:10;s:10:"image/jpeg";i:11;s:11:"public.jpeg";i:12;s:15:"Raw JPEG Stream";i:13;N;i:14;s:4:"JPEG";i:15;s:6:"fmt/41";i:16;N;i:17;s:14:"Image (Raster)";i:18;N;i:19;s:990:"The JPEG File Interchange Format (JFIF) is a file format for storing JPEG-compressed raster images. It was developed by the Independent JPEG Group and C-Cube Microsystems, in the absence of any such format being defined in the JPEG standard, and rapidly became a de facto standard; this is what is commonly referred to as the JPEG file format. A JFIF file comprises a JPEG data stream together with a JFIF marker. It begins with a Start of Image (SOI) marker, immediately followed by a JFIF Application (APP0). This is followed by the JPEG image data, which is terminated by an End of Image (EOI) marker. JFIF supports up to 24-bit colour and uses lossy compression (based on the Discrete Cosine Transform algorithm). Other types of compression are available through JPEG extensions, including progressive image buildup, arithmetic encoding, variable quantization, selective refinement, image tiling, and lossless compression, but these may not be supported by all JFIF readers and writers.";i:20;s:6:"Binary";i:21;s:21:"Big-endian (Motorola)";i:22;a:2:{i:0;s:122:"https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/PRONOM/Format/proFormatSearch.aspx?status=detailReport&id=670&strPageToDisplay=summary";i:1;s:58:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/JPEG_File_Interchange_Format";}}i:200;a:23:{i:0;i:0;i:1;i:3;i:2;s:1:"s";i:3;s:3:"‹";i:4;i:0;i:5;i:0;i:6;s:1:"s";i:7;s:0:"";i:8;s:2:"gz";i:9;a:1:{i:0;s:2:"gz";}i:10;s:18:"application/x-gzip";i:11;s:23:"org.gnu.gnu-zip-archive";i:12;s:11:"GZIP Format";i:13;N;i:14;N;i:15;s:9:"x-fmt/266";i:16;N;i:17;s:9:"Aggregate";i:18;N;i:19;s:392:"gzip is a file format and a software application used for file compression and decompression. The program was created by Jean-loup Gailly and Mark Adler as a free software replacement for the compress program used in early Unix systems, and intended for use by GNU (the "g" is from "GNU"). Version 0.1 was first publicly released on 31 October 1992, and version 1.0 followed in February 1993.";i:20;s:4:"Text";i:21;N;i:22;a:3:{i:0;s:122:"https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/PRONOM/Format/proFormatSearch.aspx?status=detailReport&id=386&strPageToDisplay=summary";i:1;s:34:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gzip";i:2;s:40:"https://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc1952.html";}}i:201;a:23:{i:0;i:0;i:1;i:3;i:2;s:1:"s";i:3;s:3:"ID3";i:4;i:0;i:5;i:0;i:6;s:1:"s";i:7;s:0:"";i:8;s:3:"mp3";i:9;a:1:{i:0;s:3:"mp3";}i:10;s:10:"audio/mpeg";i:11;s:10:"public.mp3";i:12;s:37:"MPEG 1/2 Audio Layer 3 with ID3v2 Tag";i:13;N;i:14;s:3:"MP3";i:15;N;i:16;N;i:17;s:5:"Audio";i:18;N;i:19;s:249:"MP3 is a digital audio format, formally known as MPEG-1 Audio Layer III or MPEG-2 Audio Layer III. A third variant, known as MPEG 2.5, which supports lower bit rates implemented by the LAME MP3 encoder among others, but is not a recognised standard.";i:20;N;i:21;N;i:22;a:1:{i:0;s:33:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/MP3";}}i:202;a:23:{i:0;i:0;i:1;i:3;i:2;s:2:"sr";i:3;s:7:"[CFZ]WS";i:4;i:0;i:5;i:0;i:6;s:1:"s";i:7;s:0:"";i:8;s:3:"swf";i:9;a:1:{i:0;s:3:"swf";}i:10;s:29:"application/x-shockwave-flash";i:11;N;i:12;s:22:"Small Web Format (SWF)";i:13;N;i:14;N;i:15;N;i:16;N;i:17;N;i:18;N;i:19;s:373:"SWF is an Adobe Flash file format used for multimedia, vector graphics and ActionScript. Originating with FutureWave Software, then transferred to Macromedia, and then coming under the control of Adobe, SWF files can contain animations or applets of varying degrees of interactivity and function. They may also occur in programs, commonly browser games, using ActionScript.";i:20;N;i:21;N;i:22;a:2:{i:0;s:33:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SWF";i:1;s:41:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Adobe_Flash";}}i:203;a:23:{i:0;i:0;i:1;i:2;i:2;s:2:"sr";i:3;s:18:"\xff[\xf2\xf3\xfb]";i:4;i:0;i:5;i:0;i:6;s:1:"s";i:7;s:0:"";i:8;s:3:"mp3";i:9;a:1:{i:0;s:3:"mp3";}i:10;s:10:"audio/mpeg";i:11;s:10:"public.mp3";i:12;s:40:"MPEG 1/2 Audio Layer 3 without ID3v2 Tag";i:13;N;i:14;s:3:"MP3";i:15;N;i:16;N;i:17;s:5:"Audio";i:18;N;i:19;s:249:"MP3 is a digital audio format, formally known as MPEG-1 Audio Layer III or MPEG-2 Audio Layer III. A third variant, known as MPEG 2.5, which supports lower bit rates implemented by the LAME MP3 encoder among others, but is not a recognised standard.";i:20;N;i:21;N;i:22;a:2:{i:0;s:33:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/MP3";i:1;s:53:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_file_signatures";}}i:204;a:23:{i:0;i:0;i:1;i:2;i:2;s:1:"s";i:3;s:2:"P7";i:4;i:0;i:5;i:0;i:6;s:1:"s";i:7;s:0:"";i:8;s:3:"pam";i:9;a:1:{i:0;s:3:"pam";}i:10;s:29:"image/x-portable-arbitrarymap";i:11;N;i:12;s:16:"Portable Any Map";i:13;N;i:14;N;i:15;s:7:"fmt/405";i:16;N;i:17;N;i:18;N;i:19;s:400:"Several graphics formats are used and defined by the Netpbm project. The portable pixmap format (PPM), the portable graymap format (PGM) and the portable bitmap format (PBM) are image file formats designed to be easily exchanged between platforms. They are also sometimes referred to collectively as the portable anymap format (PNM), not to be confused with the related portable arbitrary map format.";i:20;N;i:21;N;i:22;a:3:{i:0;s:123:"https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/PRONOM/Format/proFormatSearch.aspx?status=detailReport&id=1153&strPageToDisplay=summary";i:1;s:36:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Netpbm";i:2;s:42:"http://netpbm.sourceforge.net/doc/pam.html";}}i:205;a:23:{i:0;i:0;i:1;i:2;i:2;s:1:"s";i:3;s:2:"%!";i:4;i:0;i:5;i:0;i:6;s:1:"s";i:7;s:0:"";i:8;s:2:"ps";i:9;a:1:{i:0;s:2:"ps";}i:10;s:22:"application/postscript";i:11;s:20:"com.adobe.postscript";i:12;s:10:"Postscript";i:13;N;i:14;N;i:15;N;i:16;N;i:17;s:16:"Page Description";i:18;N;i:19;s:63:"Postscript is a computer language for creating vector graphics.";i:20;s:4:"Text";i:21;N;i:22;a:5:{i:0;s:40:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/PostScript";i:1;s:101:"https://web.archive.org/web/20170218093716if_/https://www.adobe.com/products/postscript/pdfs/PLRM.pdf";i:2;s:123:"https://web.archive.org/web/20160305010005if_/http://partners.adobe.com/public/developer/en/ps/PS3010and3011.Supplement.pdf";i:3;s:108:"https://web.archive.org/web/20150321034514if_/http://partners.adobe.com/public/developer/en/font/T1_SPEC.PDF";i:4;s:82:"https://w3-o.cs.hm.edu/users/ruckert/public_html/compiler/ThinkingInPostScript.pdf";}}}i:1;i:49164;i:2;i:1024;}